window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-112"]=[{"id":"K112.01","q":"112:1–4","s":112,"surah":"al-Ikhlāṣ","surah_ar":"الإخلاص","title":"al-Ikhlāṣ · Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ","cairo":"K IV:817–819 · T XVI:632–643","source_page_first":817,"source_page_last":819,"chunk":"passages-112","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ. Meccan; it is also said to be Medinan. It has four verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Say: He is God, One. God, the One to whom all turn in need (al-Ṣamad). He neither begot nor was begotten, and no one is equal to Him.” [112:1–4]\n[REVIEW K112-A: The separate four-volume transcription additionally prints “revealed after al-Nās” in its heading and repeats the basmala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">بسملة</bdi>). These are absent from the corresponding Dubai base-text heading. Their authorial status is unestablished; they are recorded rather than silently inserted.]</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K112.01"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"مكية، وقيل: مدنية، وهي أربع آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\n﴿قُلْ هُوَ اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ (1) اللَّهُ الصَّمَدُ (2) لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ (3) وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ كُفُواً أَحَدٌ﴾ [1 - 4]","layers":[{"id":"K112.01","heading":"[K112.01 | T XVI 632; K IV 817]","text":"Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ. Meccan; it is also said to be Medinan. It has four verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Say: He is God, One. God, the One to whom all turn in need (al-Ṣamad). He neither begot nor was begotten, and no one is equal to Him.” [112:1–4]\n[REVIEW K112-A: The separate four-volume transcription additionally prints “revealed after al-Nās” in its heading and repeats the basmala (بسملة). These are absent from the corresponding Dubai base-text heading. Their authorial status is unestablished; they are recorded rather than silently inserted.]","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":112,"ar":"مكية، وقيل: مدنية، وهي أربع آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\n﴿قُلْ هُوَ اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ (1) اللَّهُ الصَّمَدُ (2) لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ (3) وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ كُفُواً أَحَدٌ﴾ [1 - 4]","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ. Meccan; it is also said to be Medinan. It has four verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Say: He is God, One. God, the One to whom all turn in need (al-Ṣamad). He neither begot nor was begotten, and no one is equal to Him.” [112:1–4]\n[REVIEW K112-A: The separate four-volume transcription additionally prints “revealed after al-Nās” in its heading and repeats the basmala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">بسملة</bdi>). These are absent from the corresponding Dubai base-text heading. Their authorial status is unestablished; they are recorded rather than silently inserted.]</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true,"display_normalization":"Reversed electronic reference brackets normalized; source text preserved in the dossier."}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"112:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ","en":"Say: He is Allah, the One"},{"key":"112:2","number":2,"ar":"ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ","en":"Allah, the eternally Besought of all"},{"key":"112:3","number":3,"ar":"لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ","en":"He begetteth not nor was begotten"},{"key":"112:4","number":4,"ar":"وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ","en":"And there is none comparable unto Him"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ ﴿1﴾ ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ ﴿2﴾ لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ ﴿3﴾ وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: He is Allah, the One 2. Allah, the eternally Besought of all 3. He begetteth not nor was begotten 4. And there is none comparable unto Him"},{"id":"K112.02","q":"112:1","s":112,"surah":"al-Ikhlāṣ","surah_ar":"الإخلاص","title":"al-Ikhlāṣ · Huwa [He/it] is the pronoun of the matter (ḍamīr al-shaʾn), and “God is One” is that matter, as when","cairo":"K IV:817–819 · T XVI:632–643","source_page_first":817,"source_page_last":819,"chunk":"passages-112","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>Huwa [He/it] is the pronoun of the matter (ḍamīr al-shaʾn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضمير الشأن</bdi>)), and “God is One” is that matter, as when you say, “It is: Zayd is departing.” It is as though one had said: the matter is this—namely, that God is one, with no second beside Him.\nIf you ask, “What is the syntactic position of huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>)?”, I answer: nominative, as the subject (mubtadaʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مبتدأ</bdi>)); its predicate is the sentence.\nIf you ask, “A sentence functioning as a predicate must contain something referring back to the subject. Where, then, is that reference?”, I answer: this sentence has the same status as the single predicate in your statement “Zayd is your servant-boy”: in meaning, it is the subject itself. For “God is One” is precisely the matter expressed by huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>). This is not the case with “Zayd—his father is departing,” since Zayd and the sentence denote two different meanings; something connecting them is therefore indispensable.\nIbn ʿAbbās reports: Quraysh said, “Muḥammad, describe to us your Lord, to whom you summon us,” and the sūrah was revealed. That is: the one whose description you have asked me for is God. Aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) is a substitution (badal (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">بدل</bdi>)) for “God”; alternatively, [it is construed with an understood subject] “He is One.” It has the meaning of wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>), and its original form is waḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَحَد</bdi>).</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K112.02"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“The one whose description you asked me for is God, and aḥad is a substitution”","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ. Meccan; it is also said to be Medinan. Four verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\nHis words, “The one whose description you asked me for is God, and aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) is a substitution.” Abū al-Baqāʾ says: “Huwa is a subject meaning ‘the one asked about,’ for they said, ‘Is your Lord made of copper or gold?’ On this interpretation, Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) may be the predicate of the subject, with aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) a substitution, or the predicate of an omitted subject. Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) may also be a substitution, with aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) the predicate. The hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) of aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) replaces wāw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">و</bdi>), since it means al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>).” Substitution of hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) for a wāw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">و</bdi>) carrying fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) is rare. It is also said that the hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) is original, like the hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) in aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) used for generality.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.01"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“It has the meaning of wāḥid”","html":"<p>His words, “It has the meaning of wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>).” There are two possibilities. First, this may attach to the second interpretation: huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>) answers their request, “Describe your Lord to us,” and is the pronoun referring to the one asked about. In that case, a distinction must be drawn between wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>) and aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>). He says in al-Aḥzāb: “Aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) originally means waḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَحَد</bdi>), that is, al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>); then it was assigned to general negation, applying equally to masculine and feminine, one and more than one.”\nThe author of al-Nihāya transmits from al-Azharī: “The difference between al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>) and al-aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الأحد</bdi>) is that aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) was formed to negate whatever number is mentioned with it: you say, ‘No one came to me.’ Wāḥid is a name formed for the beginning of number: you say, ‘One person came to me,’ but do not say [in this affirmative construction], ‘Aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) came to me.’ Al-wāḥid is unique in essence, without likeness or counterpart; al-aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الأحد</bdi>) is unique in meaning. It is also said: al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>) is that which is not divisible, is not doubled, does not admit division, and has neither counterpart nor likeness. Only God Most High combines these two descriptions.”\nAl-Azharī says in Tafsīr asmāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أسماء</bdi>) Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) al-ḥusnā: “Al-aḥad is among the attributes God has reserved to Himself: nothing shares it with Him, and nothing other than God is described as al-aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الأحد</bdi>). One does not say ‘an aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) man’ or ‘an aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) dirham’; one says ‘a wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>) man.’”\nKnowing this, we say: when they said, “Describe to us your Lord, to whom you summon us,” they were told: the one asked about is God, one, unique in essence, without likeness or counterpart. The discourse thus serves to distinguish, and the description differentiates. Although it entails magnification, [when] huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>) is taken as the pronoun of the matter, the descriptions serve magnification alone. For this is the beginning of the Messenger&#39;s ﷺ mission, guiding the people and drawing attention to an object of worship of immense dignity and overwhelming dominion. It is as though one had said: Say, Muḥammad, the matter and affair is that God is One, with no second beside Him. Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) indicates all the attributes of perfection; aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>), all the attributes of majesty. Accordingly, it is appropriate to say “One, with no second beside Him,” because aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) indicates the negation of whatever is mentioned with it.\nThe second possibility is that [his explanation] attaches to both interpretations together: either huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>) is the pronoun of the matter, or huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>) means the one asked about. There would then be no distinction between aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) and wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>). Al-Jawharī says, “Al-aḥad means al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>), and is the beginning of number.” The author of al-Nihāya says, “Al-wāḥid is the solitary one who has always been alone, with no other beside Him.”\n[TEXT CORRECTIONS T112-C1: The scan at p.633 reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا يتجزأ</bdi> and <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إلا الله</bdi>, correcting the transcription. At p.634 the words <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">على أن يكون</bdi> «<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>» <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضمير الشأن، فإجراء الأوصاف لمجرد التعظيم</bdi> are present in the scan but absent in OpenITI. They are translated above. The transition following <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وإن استلزم التعظيم</bdi> remains syntactically awkward; bracketed “when” makes the understood construction explicit.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.02"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"(هُوَ) ضمير الشأن، و (اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ) هو الشأن، كقولك: هو زيد منطلق، كأنه قيل: الشأن هذا، وهو أنّ الله واحد لا ثاني له.\n\nفإن قلت: ما محل (هو)؟\n\nقلت: الرفع على الابتداء والخبر الجملة.\n\nفإن قلت: فالجملة الواقعة خبرًا لا بد فيها من راجع إلى المبتدأ، فأين الراجع؟\n\nقلت: حكم هذه الجملة حكم المفرد في قولك: (زيد غلامك) في أنه هو المبتدأ في المعنى، وذلك أن قوله: (اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ) هو الشأن الذي هو عبارة عنه، وليس كذلك (زيد أبوه منطلق)؛ فإنّ زيدًا والجملة يدلان على معنيين مختلفين، فلا بد مما يصل بينهما. وعن ابن عباس: قالت قريش: يا محمد، صف لنا ربك الذي تدعونا إليه، فنزلت، يعنى: الذي سألتموني وصفه هو الله، و (أحد): بدل من قوله: (الله)،\n\nأو على: هو أحد، وهو بمعنى واحد، وأصله: وحد\n\nمكية، وقيل مدنية، وهي أربع آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقولُه: (الذي سألتموني وَصْفَه هو الله، و ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾ بدل)، قالَ أبو البقاء: «﴿هُوَ﴾: مبتدأُ\n\nبمعنى المسؤولِ عنه؛ لأنهم قالوا: ربُّك من نحاس أم من ذهب؟ فعلى هذا: يجوزُ أن يكونَ ﴿اللَّهُ﴾ خبرَ المبتدأ، و ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾ بدل، أو خبرُ مبتدأٍ محذوفٍ. يجوزُ أن يكونَ ﴿اللَّهُ﴾ بدلًا، و ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾ الخبر. وهمزةُ ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾ بدل من الواو؛ لأنه بمعنى الواحد»، وإبدالُ الواو المفتوحةِ همزةً قليل، وقيل: الهمزةُ أصلٌ كالهمزة في ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾ المستعمل للعموم.\n\nقولُه: (وهو بمعنى واحد)، وفيه احتمالان: أحدُهما أن يتعلقَ بالوجهِ الثاني، وهو أن يكون ﴿هُوَ﴾ جوابًا عن قولهم: صِفْ لنا ربك، ولفظةُ ﴿هُوَ﴾ ضميرُ المسؤول؛ فإذن لابُدَّ من الفرقِ بين واحد وأَحد؛ قال في \" الأحزاب\": «أحدٌ في الأصلِ بمعنى وَحَدٍ، وهو الواحد، ثم وضعَ في النفيِ العامِّ مستويًا فيه المذكرُ والمؤنثُ والواحدُ وما وراءَه».\n\nوروى صاحبُ \"النهاية\" عن الأزهري أنه قال: «الفرقُ بين الواحدِ والأحدِ: أن الأحدَ بُني لنفيِ ما يُذْكَرُ معه من العدد، تقول: ما جاءني أحد، والواحدُ: اسمٌ بني لمفتتحِ العدد، تقول: جاءني واحد من الناس، ولا تقول: جاءني أحد؛ فالواحدُ منفردٌ بالذاتِ في عدمِ المثلِ والنظير، والأحدُ منفردٌ بالمعنى. وقيل: الواحدُ هو الذي لا يتجزّأ، ولا يُثنى، ولا يقبلُ الانقسام، ولا نظيرَ له ولا مِثْل، ولا يَجمعُ هذين الوصفين إلا الله تعالى».\n\nوقال الأزهريُّ في \"تفسير أسماء الله الحسنى\": «الأحدُ من صفاتِ الله التي استأثرَ اللهُ بها، فلايشركه فيها شئٌ، ولا يوصفُ شئٌ بالأحدِ غيرُ الله؛ لا يقال: رجلٌ احدٌ، ولا درهمٌ أحدٌ؛ وإنما يقال: رجلٌ واحدٌ».\n\nإذا عُلِمَ هذا، فنقول: إنهم لما قالوا: صِفْ لنا ربَّك الذي تدعونا إليه، قيل لهم: المسؤول عنه الله، وهو واحدٌ متفردٌ بالذاتِ في عدم المثلِ والنظير؛ فإجراءُ الكلامِ للتمييز، والصّفةُ فارقة.\n\nوإن استلزمَ التعظيم، على أن يكون «هو» ضمير الشأن، فإجراء الأوصاف لمجرد التعظيم؛ لأنه ابتداءُ أمرِ الرسول صلى الله عليه وسلم، إرشادًا للقوم، وتنبيهًا على معبودٍ عظيمِ الشأنِ قاهرِ السلطان، فكأنه قيل: قُلْ يا محمدُ: الشأنُ والأمرُ أن الله أحدٌ لا ثاني له، فَدلَّ بقوله: ﴿اللَّهُ﴾، على جميعِ صفاتِ الكمال، وبالأحدِ على جميع صفاتِ الجلال؛ فالمناسبُ أن يقالَ: واحدٌ لا ثاني له، لأنه دالٌّ لنفيِ ما يُذكرُ معه.\n\nوالاحتمالُ الثاني، وهو أن يتعلَّق بالوجهينِ كليهما، أي: ﴿هُوَ﴾ ضميرُ الشأن، أو ﴿هُوَ﴾ بمعنى المسؤول؛ فحينئذٍ لا فرق بين أحدٍ وواحد، قالَ الجوهري: «الأحدُ بمعنى الواحد، وهو أول العدد»، وقال صاحب \"النهاية\": «الواحدُ هو الفردُ الذي لم يزل وحدَه، ولم يكن معه آخر».","layers":[{"id":"K112.02","heading":"[K112.02 | T XVI 632–633; K IV 817]","text":"Huwa [He/it] is the pronoun of the matter (ḍamīr al-shaʾn (ضمير الشأن)), and “God is One” is that matter, as when you say, “It is: Zayd is departing.” It is as though one had said: the matter is this—namely, that God is one, with no second beside Him.\nIf you ask, “What is the syntactic position of huwa (هو)?”, I answer: nominative, as the subject (mubtadaʾ (مبتدأ)); its predicate is the sentence.\nIf you ask, “A sentence functioning as a predicate must contain something referring back to the subject. Where, then, is that reference?”, I answer: this sentence has the same status as the single predicate in your statement “Zayd is your servant-boy”: in meaning, it is the subject itself. For “God is One” is precisely the matter expressed by huwa (هو). This is not the case with “Zayd—his father is departing,” since Zayd and the sentence denote two different meanings; something connecting them is therefore indispensable.\nIbn ʿAbbās reports: Quraysh said, “Muḥammad, describe to us your Lord, to whom you summon us,” and the sūrah was revealed. That is: the one whose description you have asked me for is God. Aḥad (أحد) is a substitution (badal (بدل)) for “God”; alternatively, [it is construed with an understood subject] “He is One.” It has the meaning of wāḥid (واحد), and its original form is waḥad (وَحَد).","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":112,"ar":"(هُوَ) ضمير الشأن، و (اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ) هو الشأن، كقولك: هو زيد منطلق، كأنه قيل: الشأن هذا، وهو أنّ الله واحد لا ثاني له.\n\nفإن قلت: ما محل (هو)؟\n\nقلت: الرفع على الابتداء والخبر الجملة.\n\nفإن قلت: فالجملة الواقعة خبرًا لا بد فيها من راجع إلى المبتدأ، فأين الراجع؟\n\nقلت: حكم هذه الجملة حكم المفرد في قولك: (زيد غلامك) في أنه هو المبتدأ في المعنى، وذلك أن قوله: (اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ) هو الشأن الذي هو عبارة عنه، وليس كذلك (زيد أبوه منطلق)؛ فإنّ زيدًا والجملة يدلان على معنيين مختلفين، فلا بد مما يصل بينهما. وعن ابن عباس: قالت قريش: يا محمد، صف لنا ربك الذي تدعونا إليه، فنزلت، يعنى: الذي سألتموني وصفه هو الله، و (أحد): بدل من قوله: (الله)،\n\nأو على: هو أحد، وهو بمعنى واحد، وأصله: وحد","html":"<p>Huwa [He/it] is the pronoun of the matter (ḍamīr al-shaʾn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضمير الشأن</bdi>)), and “God is One” is that matter, as when you say, “It is: Zayd is departing.” It is as though one had said: the matter is this—namely, that God is one, with no second beside Him.\nIf you ask, “What is the syntactic position of huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>)?”, I answer: nominative, as the subject (mubtadaʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مبتدأ</bdi>)); its predicate is the sentence.\nIf you ask, “A sentence functioning as a predicate must contain something referring back to the subject. Where, then, is that reference?”, I answer: this sentence has the same status as the single predicate in your statement “Zayd is your servant-boy”: in meaning, it is the subject itself. For “God is One” is precisely the matter expressed by huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>). This is not the case with “Zayd—his father is departing,” since Zayd and the sentence denote two different meanings; something connecting them is therefore indispensable.\nIbn ʿAbbās reports: Quraysh said, “Muḥammad, describe to us your Lord, to whom you summon us,” and the sūrah was revealed. That is: the one whose description you have asked me for is God. Aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) is a substitution (badal (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">بدل</bdi>)) for “God”; alternatively, [it is construed with an understood subject] “He is One.” It has the meaning of wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>), and its original form is waḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَحَد</bdi>).</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.01","heading":"[T112.01 | XVI 632–633 | on K112.02]","text":"Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ. Meccan; it is also said to be Medinan. Four verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\nHis words, “The one whose description you asked me for is God, and aḥad (أحد) is a substitution.” Abū al-Baqāʾ says: “Huwa is a subject meaning ‘the one asked about,’ for they said, ‘Is your Lord made of copper or gold?’ On this interpretation, Allāh (الله) may be the predicate of the subject, with aḥad (أحد) a substitution, or the predicate of an omitted subject. Allāh (الله) may also be a substitution, with aḥad (أحد) the predicate. The hamza (همزة) of aḥad (أحد) replaces wāw (و), since it means al-wāḥid (الواحد).” Substitution of hamza (همزة) for a wāw (و) carrying fatḥa (فَتْحة) is rare. It is also said that the hamza (همزة) is original, like the hamza (همزة) in aḥad (أحد) used for generality.","title":"“The one whose description you asked me for is God, and aḥad is a substitution”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.02"],"anchor":"K112.02","surah":112,"ar":"مكية، وقيل مدنية، وهي أربع آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقولُه: (الذي سألتموني وَصْفَه هو الله، و ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾ بدل)، قالَ أبو البقاء: «﴿هُوَ﴾: مبتدأُ\n\nبمعنى المسؤولِ عنه؛ لأنهم قالوا: ربُّك من نحاس أم من ذهب؟ فعلى هذا: يجوزُ أن يكونَ ﴿اللَّهُ﴾ خبرَ المبتدأ، و ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾ بدل، أو خبرُ مبتدأٍ محذوفٍ. يجوزُ أن يكونَ ﴿اللَّهُ﴾ بدلًا، و ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾ الخبر. وهمزةُ ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾ بدل من الواو؛ لأنه بمعنى الواحد»، وإبدالُ الواو المفتوحةِ همزةً قليل، وقيل: الهمزةُ أصلٌ كالهمزة في ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾ المستعمل للعموم.","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ. Meccan; it is also said to be Medinan. Four verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\nHis words, “The one whose description you asked me for is God, and aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) is a substitution.” Abū al-Baqāʾ says: “Huwa is a subject meaning ‘the one asked about,’ for they said, ‘Is your Lord made of copper or gold?’ On this interpretation, Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) may be the predicate of the subject, with aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) a substitution, or the predicate of an omitted subject. Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) may also be a substitution, with aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) the predicate. The hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) of aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) replaces wāw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">و</bdi>), since it means al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>).” Substitution of hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) for a wāw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">و</bdi>) carrying fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) is rare. It is also said that the hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) is original, like the hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) in aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) used for generality.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.02","heading":"[T112.02 | XVI 633–634 | on K112.02]","text":"His words, “It has the meaning of wāḥid (واحد).” There are two possibilities. First, this may attach to the second interpretation: huwa (هو) answers their request, “Describe your Lord to us,” and is the pronoun referring to the one asked about. In that case, a distinction must be drawn between wāḥid (واحد) and aḥad (أحد). He says in al-Aḥzāb: “Aḥad (أحد) originally means waḥad (وَحَد), that is, al-wāḥid (الواحد); then it was assigned to general negation, applying equally to masculine and feminine, one and more than one.”\nThe author of al-Nihāya transmits from al-Azharī: “The difference between al-wāḥid (الواحد) and al-aḥad (الأحد) is that aḥad (أحد) was formed to negate whatever number is mentioned with it: you say, ‘No one came to me.’ Wāḥid is a name formed for the beginning of number: you say, ‘One person came to me,’ but do not say [in this affirmative construction], ‘Aḥad (أحد) came to me.’ Al-wāḥid is unique in essence, without likeness or counterpart; al-aḥad (الأحد) is unique in meaning. It is also said: al-wāḥid (الواحد) is that which is not divisible, is not doubled, does not admit division, and has neither counterpart nor likeness. Only God Most High combines these two descriptions.”\nAl-Azharī says in Tafsīr asmāʾ (أسماء) Allāh (الله) al-ḥusnā: “Al-aḥad is among the attributes God has reserved to Himself: nothing shares it with Him, and nothing other than God is described as al-aḥad (الأحد). One does not say ‘an aḥad (أحد) man’ or ‘an aḥad (أحد) dirham’; one says ‘a wāḥid (واحد) man.’”\nKnowing this, we say: when they said, “Describe to us your Lord, to whom you summon us,” they were told: the one asked about is God, one, unique in essence, without likeness or counterpart. The discourse thus serves to distinguish, and the description differentiates. Although it entails magnification, [when] huwa (هو) is taken as the pronoun of the matter, the descriptions serve magnification alone. For this is the beginning of the Messenger's ﷺ mission, guiding the people and drawing attention to an object of worship of immense dignity and overwhelming dominion. It is as though one had said: Say, Muḥammad, the matter and affair is that God is One, with no second beside Him. Allāh (الله) indicates all the attributes of perfection; aḥad (أحد), all the attributes of majesty. Accordingly, it is appropriate to say “One, with no second beside Him,” because aḥad (أحد) indicates the negation of whatever is mentioned with it.\nThe second possibility is that [his explanation] attaches to both interpretations together: either huwa (هو) is the pronoun of the matter, or huwa (هو) means the one asked about. There would then be no distinction between aḥad (أحد) and wāḥid (واحد). Al-Jawharī says, “Al-aḥad means al-wāḥid (الواحد), and is the beginning of number.” The author of al-Nihāya says, “Al-wāḥid is the solitary one who has always been alone, with no other beside Him.”\n[TEXT CORRECTIONS T112-C1: The scan at p.633 reads لا يتجزأ and إلا الله, correcting the transcription. At p.634 the words على أن يكون «هو» ضمير الشأن، فإجراء الأوصاف لمجرد التعظيم are present in the scan but absent in OpenITI. They are translated above. The transition following وإن استلزم التعظيم remains syntactically awkward; bracketed “when” makes the understood construction explicit.]","title":"“It has the meaning of wāḥid”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.02"],"anchor":"K112.02","surah":112,"ar":"قولُه: (وهو بمعنى واحد)، وفيه احتمالان: أحدُهما أن يتعلقَ بالوجهِ الثاني، وهو أن يكون ﴿هُوَ﴾ جوابًا عن قولهم: صِفْ لنا ربك، ولفظةُ ﴿هُوَ﴾ ضميرُ المسؤول؛ فإذن لابُدَّ من الفرقِ بين واحد وأَحد؛ قال في \" الأحزاب\": «أحدٌ في الأصلِ بمعنى وَحَدٍ، وهو الواحد، ثم وضعَ في النفيِ العامِّ مستويًا فيه المذكرُ والمؤنثُ والواحدُ وما وراءَه».\n\nوروى صاحبُ \"النهاية\" عن الأزهري أنه قال: «الفرقُ بين الواحدِ والأحدِ: أن الأحدَ بُني لنفيِ ما يُذْكَرُ معه من العدد، تقول: ما جاءني أحد، والواحدُ: اسمٌ بني لمفتتحِ العدد، تقول: جاءني واحد من الناس، ولا تقول: جاءني أحد؛ فالواحدُ منفردٌ بالذاتِ في عدمِ المثلِ والنظير، والأحدُ منفردٌ بالمعنى. وقيل: الواحدُ هو الذي لا يتجزّأ، ولا يُثنى، ولا يقبلُ الانقسام، ولا نظيرَ له ولا مِثْل، ولا يَجمعُ هذين الوصفين إلا الله تعالى».\n\nوقال الأزهريُّ في \"تفسير أسماء الله الحسنى\": «الأحدُ من صفاتِ الله التي استأثرَ اللهُ بها، فلايشركه فيها شئٌ، ولا يوصفُ شئٌ بالأحدِ غيرُ الله؛ لا يقال: رجلٌ احدٌ، ولا درهمٌ أحدٌ؛ وإنما يقال: رجلٌ واحدٌ».\n\nإذا عُلِمَ هذا، فنقول: إنهم لما قالوا: صِفْ لنا ربَّك الذي تدعونا إليه، قيل لهم: المسؤول عنه الله، وهو واحدٌ متفردٌ بالذاتِ في عدم المثلِ والنظير؛ فإجراءُ الكلامِ للتمييز، والصّفةُ فارقة.\n\nوإن استلزمَ التعظيم، على أن يكون «هو» ضمير الشأن، فإجراء الأوصاف لمجرد التعظيم؛ لأنه ابتداءُ أمرِ الرسول صلى الله عليه وسلم، إرشادًا للقوم، وتنبيهًا على معبودٍ عظيمِ الشأنِ قاهرِ السلطان، فكأنه قيل: قُلْ يا محمدُ: الشأنُ والأمرُ أن الله أحدٌ لا ثاني له، فَدلَّ بقوله: ﴿اللَّهُ﴾، على جميعِ صفاتِ الكمال، وبالأحدِ على جميع صفاتِ الجلال؛ فالمناسبُ أن يقالَ: واحدٌ لا ثاني له، لأنه دالٌّ لنفيِ ما يُذكرُ معه.\n\nوالاحتمالُ الثاني، وهو أن يتعلَّق بالوجهينِ كليهما، أي: ﴿هُوَ﴾ ضميرُ الشأن، أو ﴿هُوَ﴾ بمعنى المسؤول؛ فحينئذٍ لا فرق بين أحدٍ وواحد، قالَ الجوهري: «الأحدُ بمعنى الواحد، وهو أول العدد»، وقال صاحب \"النهاية\": «الواحدُ هو الفردُ الذي لم يزل وحدَه، ولم يكن معه آخر».","html":"<p>His words, “It has the meaning of wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>).” There are two possibilities. First, this may attach to the second interpretation: huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>) answers their request, “Describe your Lord to us,” and is the pronoun referring to the one asked about. In that case, a distinction must be drawn between wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>) and aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>). He says in al-Aḥzāb: “Aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) originally means waḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَحَد</bdi>), that is, al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>); then it was assigned to general negation, applying equally to masculine and feminine, one and more than one.”\nThe author of al-Nihāya transmits from al-Azharī: “The difference between al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>) and al-aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الأحد</bdi>) is that aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) was formed to negate whatever number is mentioned with it: you say, ‘No one came to me.’ Wāḥid is a name formed for the beginning of number: you say, ‘One person came to me,’ but do not say [in this affirmative construction], ‘Aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) came to me.’ Al-wāḥid is unique in essence, without likeness or counterpart; al-aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الأحد</bdi>) is unique in meaning. It is also said: al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>) is that which is not divisible, is not doubled, does not admit division, and has neither counterpart nor likeness. Only God Most High combines these two descriptions.”\nAl-Azharī says in Tafsīr asmāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أسماء</bdi>) Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) al-ḥusnā: “Al-aḥad is among the attributes God has reserved to Himself: nothing shares it with Him, and nothing other than God is described as al-aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الأحد</bdi>). One does not say ‘an aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) man’ or ‘an aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) dirham’; one says ‘a wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>) man.’”\nKnowing this, we say: when they said, “Describe to us your Lord, to whom you summon us,” they were told: the one asked about is God, one, unique in essence, without likeness or counterpart. The discourse thus serves to distinguish, and the description differentiates. Although it entails magnification, [when] huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>) is taken as the pronoun of the matter, the descriptions serve magnification alone. For this is the beginning of the Messenger&#39;s ﷺ mission, guiding the people and drawing attention to an object of worship of immense dignity and overwhelming dominion. It is as though one had said: Say, Muḥammad, the matter and affair is that God is One, with no second beside Him. Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) indicates all the attributes of perfection; aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>), all the attributes of majesty. Accordingly, it is appropriate to say “One, with no second beside Him,” because aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) indicates the negation of whatever is mentioned with it.\nThe second possibility is that [his explanation] attaches to both interpretations together: either huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>) is the pronoun of the matter, or huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>) means the one asked about. There would then be no distinction between aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) and wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>). Al-Jawharī says, “Al-aḥad means al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>), and is the beginning of number.” The author of al-Nihāya says, “Al-wāḥid is the solitary one who has always been alone, with no other beside Him.”\n[TEXT CORRECTIONS T112-C1: The scan at p.633 reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لا يتجزأ</bdi> and <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إلا الله</bdi>, correcting the transcription. At p.634 the words <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">على أن يكون</bdi> «<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>» <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضمير الشأن، فإجراء الأوصاف لمجرد التعظيم</bdi> are present in the scan but absent in OpenITI. They are translated above. The transition following <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وإن استلزم التعظيم</bdi> remains syntactically awkward; bracketed “when” makes the understood construction explicit.]</p>","lemma":"وهو بمعنى واحد","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"112:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ","en":"Say: He is Allah, the One"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ ﴿1﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: He is Allah, the One"},{"id":"K112.03","q":"112:1–2","s":112,"surah":"al-Ikhlāṣ","surah_ar":"الإخلاص","title":"al-Ikhlāṣ · ʿAbd Allāh and Ubayy read “Huwa Allāhu aḥad,” without “Say","cairo":"K IV:817–819 · T XVI:632–643","source_page_first":817,"source_page_last":819,"chunk":"passages-112","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>ʿAbd Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) and Ubayy read “Huwa Allāhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>),” without “Say.” In the Prophet&#39;s ﷺ reading it is “Allāhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>),” without “Say: He”; and he said, “Whoever recites ‘Allāhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>)’—it is equivalent to the Qurʾān.” Al-Aʿmash read “Qul huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>) Allāhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>).” It has also been read “aḥadu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحدُ</bdi>) llāhu,” without tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>): the tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>) is dropped because it meets the lām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ل</bdi>) of the definite article. Comparable to this is:\n“Nor remembering God, except a little.”\nThe sounder usage is to retain the tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>), giving it a kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) because of the meeting of two unvocalized consonants.\nAl-Ṣamad is on the pattern faʿal (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَعَل</bdi>) with the meaning of a passive participle (mafʿūl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مفعول</bdi>)), from ṣamada (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صمد</bdi>) ilayhi, “he directed himself towards him.” It is the master to whom people turn with their needs.\n[REVIEW K112-B: The report about equivalence is translated as given; Ṭībī&#39;s correction to “one third” follows in T112.12. Its presence here is not a fresh authentication.]</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K112.03"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“It was equivalent to the Qurʾān”","html":"<p>His words, “It was equivalent to the Qurʾān.” It has been said: its recitation was equivalent to reciting the Qurʾān. The report is cited as evidence for this reading. Perhaps the meaning is that “Say: He” functions as an introduction and preparation for “God is One.” This works only if the pronoun is taken as the pronoun of the matter.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.03"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“Nor remembering God, except a little”","html":"<p>His words, “Nor remembering God, except a little.” Its opening is:\n“I found him not returning to goodwill under reproach.”\nThat is: I reminded him. “Nor remembering” is intended with tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>); it was dropped because two unvocalized consonants met. Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) consequently remains accusative, not genitive through annexation (iḍāfa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إضافة</bdi>)). Dhākir is genitive, coordinated with mustaʿtib (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مستعتب</bdi>): “nor remembering.” That is: I reminded him of the affection formerly between us, but he was found not to turn back, through reproach, from the ugliness of what he had done.\nHis words, “The sounder usage is tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>)”: this is the well-known reading.\nHis words, “The master to whom people turn with their needs.” Al-Zajjāj cited al-Asadī:\n“Early came the death-announcer with news of the two best of Banū Asad:\nʿAmr ibn Masʿūd and the master to whom all turn.”\nAl-Ṣamad: that is, the one to whom everything turns—the one who created all things, and whom nothing can do without. Muḥyī al-Sunna transmitted from Ibn ʿAbbās, Mujāhid, al-Ḥasan and Saʿīd ibn Jubayr: “Al-Ṣamad is the one with no interior cavity.” Al-Shaʿbī said: “The one who neither eats nor drinks.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.04"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"وقرأ عبد الله وأبىّ: (هو الله أحد) بغير (قُلْ)، وفي قراءة النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: (الله أحد) بغير (قُلْ هُوَ)، وقال: \"من قرأ: الله أحد، كان بعدل القرآن). وقرأ الأعمش: (قل هو الله الواحد). وقرئ: (أحد الله) بغير تنوين؛\n\nأسقط لملاقاته لام التعريف. ونحوه:\n\nولا ذاكر الله إلّا قليلا\n\nوالجيد هو التنوين، وكسره لالتقاء الساكنين. و (الصَّمَدُ) فعل بمعنى مفعول، من صمد إليه إذا قصده، وهو السيد المصمود إليه في الحوائج\n\nقولُه: (كانَ يَعْدلُ القرآن)، قيل: كان قراءتُه يَعْدلُ قراءةَ القرآن، والحديث استشهادٌ لهذه القراءة. ولعلّ المرادَ أن قولَه: ﴿قُلْ هُوَ﴾ كالمقدمةِ والتمهيدِ لقوله ﴿اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ﴾، وهو إنما يستقيمُ على جَعْلِ الضميرِ للشأن.\n\nقولُه: (ولا ذاكِرِ اللهَ إلا قليلًا)، أولُه:\n\nفأَلفيتُه غيرَ مستعتبٍ\n\nأي: ذَكّرْتُه. أي: ولا ذاكر، على إرادةِ التنوين، فحذفَ لالتقاءِ الساكنين، فبقى \"الله\" منصوبًا لا مجرورًا للإضافةِ. و\"ذاكر\" جُرَّ عطفًا على \"مُسْتعتِبٍ\"، أي: ولا ذاكرٍ. أي: ذكّرتُه ما كانَ بيننا من المودةِ، فوجدَ غيرَ راجعٍ بالعتابِ من قُبحِ ما فَعَل.\n\nقولُه: (والجيّدُ هو التنوين)، وهي المشهورة.\n\nقولُه: (وهو السَّيدُ المصمودُ إليه في الحوائج)، وأنشدَ الزجاجُ للأسدي:\n\nلقد بَكَّرِ الناعي بِخَيْرَي بني أَسَد بعمرِو بنِ مسعودٍ وبالسيِّدِ الصَّمد\n\nالصَّمد: أي يصمدُ إليه كلُّ شئٍ، أي: الذي خلقَ الأشياء كلَّها، لا يستغنى عنه شئٌ.\n\nروى محيي السُّنة عن ابنِ عباسٍ ومجاهدٍ والحسنِ وسعيدِ بنِ جُبير: «الصَّمَدُ: الذي لا جَوْفَ له، وقال الشعبي: الذي لا يأكلُ ولا يشربُ».","layers":[{"id":"K112.03","heading":"[K112.03 | T XVI 634–635; K IV 817–818]","text":"ʿAbd Allāh (الله) and Ubayy read “Huwa Allāhu (الله) aḥad (أحد),” without “Say.” In the Prophet's ﷺ reading it is “Allāhu (الله) aḥad (أحد),” without “Say: He”; and he said, “Whoever recites ‘Allāhu (الله) aḥad (أحد)’—it is equivalent to the Qurʾān.” Al-Aʿmash read “Qul huwa (هو) Allāhu (الله) al-wāḥid (الواحد).” It has also been read “aḥadu (أحدُ) llāhu,” without tanwīn (تنوين): the tanwīn (تنوين) is dropped because it meets the lām (ل) of the definite article. Comparable to this is:\n“Nor remembering God, except a little.”\nThe sounder usage is to retain the tanwīn (تنوين), giving it a kasra (كَسْرة) because of the meeting of two unvocalized consonants.\nAl-Ṣamad is on the pattern faʿal (فَعَل) with the meaning of a passive participle (mafʿūl (مفعول)), from ṣamada (صمد) ilayhi, “he directed himself towards him.” It is the master to whom people turn with their needs.\n[REVIEW K112-B: The report about equivalence is translated as given; Ṭībī's correction to “one third” follows in T112.12. Its presence here is not a fresh authentication.]","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":112,"ar":"وقرأ عبد الله وأبىّ: (هو الله أحد) بغير (قُلْ)، وفي قراءة النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: (الله أحد) بغير (قُلْ هُوَ)، وقال: \"من قرأ: الله أحد، كان بعدل القرآن). وقرأ الأعمش: (قل هو الله الواحد). وقرئ: (أحد الله) بغير تنوين؛\n\nأسقط لملاقاته لام التعريف. ونحوه:\n\nولا ذاكر الله إلّا قليلا\n\nوالجيد هو التنوين، وكسره لالتقاء الساكنين. و (الصَّمَدُ) فعل بمعنى مفعول، من صمد إليه إذا قصده، وهو السيد المصمود إليه في الحوائج","html":"<p>ʿAbd Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) and Ubayy read “Huwa Allāhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>),” without “Say.” In the Prophet&#39;s ﷺ reading it is “Allāhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>),” without “Say: He”; and he said, “Whoever recites ‘Allāhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>)’—it is equivalent to the Qurʾān.” Al-Aʿmash read “Qul huwa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">هو</bdi>) Allāhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) al-wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الواحد</bdi>).” It has also been read “aḥadu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحدُ</bdi>) llāhu,” without tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>): the tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>) is dropped because it meets the lām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ل</bdi>) of the definite article. Comparable to this is:\n“Nor remembering God, except a little.”\nThe sounder usage is to retain the tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>), giving it a kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) because of the meeting of two unvocalized consonants.\nAl-Ṣamad is on the pattern faʿal (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَعَل</bdi>) with the meaning of a passive participle (mafʿūl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مفعول</bdi>)), from ṣamada (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صمد</bdi>) ilayhi, “he directed himself towards him.” It is the master to whom people turn with their needs.\n[REVIEW K112-B: The report about equivalence is translated as given; Ṭībī&#39;s correction to “one third” follows in T112.12. Its presence here is not a fresh authentication.]</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.03","heading":"[T112.03 | XVI 634 | on K112.03]","text":"His words, “It was equivalent to the Qurʾān.” It has been said: its recitation was equivalent to reciting the Qurʾān. The report is cited as evidence for this reading. Perhaps the meaning is that “Say: He” functions as an introduction and preparation for “God is One.” This works only if the pronoun is taken as the pronoun of the matter.","title":"“It was equivalent to the Qurʾān”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.03"],"anchor":"K112.03","surah":112,"ar":"قولُه: (كانَ يَعْدلُ القرآن)، قيل: كان قراءتُه يَعْدلُ قراءةَ القرآن، والحديث استشهادٌ لهذه القراءة. ولعلّ المرادَ أن قولَه: ﴿قُلْ هُوَ﴾ كالمقدمةِ والتمهيدِ لقوله ﴿اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ﴾، وهو إنما يستقيمُ على جَعْلِ الضميرِ للشأن.","html":"<p>His words, “It was equivalent to the Qurʾān.” It has been said: its recitation was equivalent to reciting the Qurʾān. The report is cited as evidence for this reading. Perhaps the meaning is that “Say: He” functions as an introduction and preparation for “God is One.” This works only if the pronoun is taken as the pronoun of the matter.</p>","lemma":"كان يعدل القرآن","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.04","heading":"[T112.04 | XVI 635 | on K112.03]","text":"His words, “Nor remembering God, except a little.” Its opening is:\n“I found him not returning to goodwill under reproach.”\nThat is: I reminded him. “Nor remembering” is intended with tanwīn (تنوين); it was dropped because two unvocalized consonants met. Allāh (الله) consequently remains accusative, not genitive through annexation (iḍāfa (إضافة)). Dhākir is genitive, coordinated with mustaʿtib (مستعتب): “nor remembering.” That is: I reminded him of the affection formerly between us, but he was found not to turn back, through reproach, from the ugliness of what he had done.\nHis words, “The sounder usage is tanwīn (تنوين)”: this is the well-known reading.\nHis words, “The master to whom people turn with their needs.” Al-Zajjāj cited al-Asadī:\n“Early came the death-announcer with news of the two best of Banū Asad:\nʿAmr ibn Masʿūd and the master to whom all turn.”\nAl-Ṣamad: that is, the one to whom everything turns—the one who created all things, and whom nothing can do without. Muḥyī al-Sunna transmitted from Ibn ʿAbbās, Mujāhid, al-Ḥasan and Saʿīd ibn Jubayr: “Al-Ṣamad is the one with no interior cavity.” Al-Shaʿbī said: “The one who neither eats nor drinks.”","title":"“Nor remembering God, except a little”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.03"],"anchor":"K112.03","surah":112,"ar":"قولُه: (ولا ذاكِرِ اللهَ إلا قليلًا)، أولُه:\n\nفأَلفيتُه غيرَ مستعتبٍ\n\nأي: ذَكّرْتُه. أي: ولا ذاكر، على إرادةِ التنوين، فحذفَ لالتقاءِ الساكنين، فبقى \"الله\" منصوبًا لا مجرورًا للإضافةِ. و\"ذاكر\" جُرَّ عطفًا على \"مُسْتعتِبٍ\"، أي: ولا ذاكرٍ. أي: ذكّرتُه ما كانَ بيننا من المودةِ، فوجدَ غيرَ راجعٍ بالعتابِ من قُبحِ ما فَعَل.\n\nقولُه: (والجيّدُ هو التنوين)، وهي المشهورة.\n\nقولُه: (وهو السَّيدُ المصمودُ إليه في الحوائج)، وأنشدَ الزجاجُ للأسدي:\n\nلقد بَكَّرِ الناعي بِخَيْرَي بني أَسَد بعمرِو بنِ مسعودٍ وبالسيِّدِ الصَّمد\n\nالصَّمد: أي يصمدُ إليه كلُّ شئٍ، أي: الذي خلقَ الأشياء كلَّها، لا يستغنى عنه شئٌ.\n\nروى محيي السُّنة عن ابنِ عباسٍ ومجاهدٍ والحسنِ وسعيدِ بنِ جُبير: «الصَّمَدُ: الذي لا جَوْفَ له، وقال الشعبي: الذي لا يأكلُ ولا يشربُ».","html":"<p>His words, “Nor remembering God, except a little.” Its opening is:\n“I found him not returning to goodwill under reproach.”\nThat is: I reminded him. “Nor remembering” is intended with tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>); it was dropped because two unvocalized consonants met. Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) consequently remains accusative, not genitive through annexation (iḍāfa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إضافة</bdi>)). Dhākir is genitive, coordinated with mustaʿtib (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مستعتب</bdi>): “nor remembering.” That is: I reminded him of the affection formerly between us, but he was found not to turn back, through reproach, from the ugliness of what he had done.\nHis words, “The sounder usage is tanwīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تنوين</bdi>)”: this is the well-known reading.\nHis words, “The master to whom people turn with their needs.” Al-Zajjāj cited al-Asadī:\n“Early came the death-announcer with news of the two best of Banū Asad:\nʿAmr ibn Masʿūd and the master to whom all turn.”\nAl-Ṣamad: that is, the one to whom everything turns—the one who created all things, and whom nothing can do without. Muḥyī al-Sunna transmitted from Ibn ʿAbbās, Mujāhid, al-Ḥasan and Saʿīd ibn Jubayr: “Al-Ṣamad is the one with no interior cavity.” Al-Shaʿbī said: “The one who neither eats nor drinks.”</p>","lemma":"ولا ذاكر الله إلا قليلا","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"112:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ","en":"Say: He is Allah, the One"},{"key":"112:2","number":2,"ar":"ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ","en":"Allah, the eternally Besought of all"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ ﴿1﴾ ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ ﴿2﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: He is Allah, the One 2. Allah, the eternally Besought of all"},{"id":"K112.04","q":"112:1–4","s":112,"surah":"al-Ikhlāṣ","surah_ar":"الإخلاص","title":"al-Ikhlāṣ · The meaning is: He is the God whom you know, and whom you acknowledge as the creator of the heavens ","cairo":"K IV:817–819 · T XVI:632–643","source_page_first":817,"source_page_last":819,"chunk":"passages-112","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>The meaning is: He is the God whom you know, and whom you acknowledge as the creator of the heavens and the earth and your own creator. He is one, alone in divinity, with no partner in it. He is the one to whom every creature turns; they cannot dispense with Him, whereas He has no need of them.\n“He did not beget,” because He shares no genus with anything, such that He might have a consort of His own genus and the two might produce offspring. His statement “How could He have a child, when He has no consort?” [6:101] indicates this meaning. “Nor was He begotten,” because everything begotten is originated and a body; He, however, is pre-eternal (qadīm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">قديم</bdi>)), with no beginning to His existence, and is not a body. No one equals Him: that is, no one resembles Him or is like Him. It is also possible to take this from suitability between spouses (kafāʾa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كفاءة</bdi>)) in marriage, thereby denying Him a consort.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K112.04"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“His statement ‘How could He have a child, when He has no consort?’ indicates this meaning”","html":"<p>Al-Rāghib: “That which has no interior cavity is of two kinds: what is below the human being, such as inanimate things; and what is above, namely the Creator, exalted and holy. The purpose of al-Ṣamad is to indicate that He differs from those to whom they ascribed divinity. Something of this kind is indicated by ‘His mother was a woman of truth; they both used to eat food’ [5:75].”\nHis words, “His statement ‘How could He have a child, when He has no consort?’ indicates this meaning”: these are coordinated with “because He shares no genus.” That is, “He did not beget” either indirectly signifies (kināya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كناية</bdi>)) His transcendence above belonging to a genus—for whoever shares a genus with anything takes a consort of his genus, and whoever takes a consort produces offspring—or [the reasoning proceeds] in reverse: how could He have a child when He has taken no consort? Generation occurs only between two partners of one genus. He transcends having a congener, so a consort cannot properly be ascribed to Him, and neither can generation. He states this in explaining the verse in al-Anʿām.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.05"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"والمعنى: هو الله الذي تعرفونه وتقرّون بأنه خالق السماوات والأرض وخالقكم، وهو واحد متوحد بالإلهية لا يشارك فيها، وهو الذي يصمد إليه كل مخلوق ولا يستغنون عنه، وهو الغنى عنهم. (لَمْ يَلِدْ) لأنه لا يجانس، حتى يكون له من جنسه صاحبة فيتوالدا. وقد دل على هذا المعنى بقوله: (أَنَّى يَكُونُ لَهُ وَلَدٌ وَلَمْ تَكُنْ لَهُ صاحِبَةٌ) [ الأنعام: 101 ]. (وَلَمْ يُولَدْ) لأنّ كل مولود محدث وجسم، وهو قديم لا أوّل لوجوده وليس بجسم ولم يكافئه أحد، أي: لم يماثله ولم يشاكله.\n\nويجوز أن يكون من الكفاءة في النكاح، نفيًا للصاحبة:\n\nالراغب: «الذي ليسَ بأجوفٍ، شيئان: أدونُ من الإنسانِ كالجمادات، وأعلى وهو الباري تعالى وتَقدّس. والقصدُ بقوله ﴿الصَّمَدُ﴾، تنبيهٌ أنه بخلافِ مَن أثبتوا له الإلهية، وإلى نحوِ هذا أشار بقولِه: ﴿وَأُمُّهُ صِدِّيقَةٌ كَانَا يَاكُلَانِ الطَّعَامَ﴾ [المائدة: 75]».\n\nقولُه: (وقَدْ دَلَّ على هذا المعنى بقوله: ﴿أَنَّى يَكُونُ لَهُ وَلَدٌ وَلَمْ تَكُنْ لَهُ صَاحِبَةٌ﴾ [الأنعام: 101]، عطفٌ على قوله: (لأنه لا يُجانَس)، يعني: ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ﴾: إما كنايةٌ عن كونه تعالى متعاليًا عن الجنسية؛ لأن مَن جانسَ شيئًا اتّخذ من جنسِه صاحبةً، ومَن اتخذَ صاحبةٍ حصَل التوالدُ.\n\nأو بالعكس بأن يقال: كيف يكونُ له ولدٌ، وأنه ما اتخذ صاحبةً؟ لأن الولادةَ لا تكونُ إلّا بين زوجين من جنسٍ واحد، وهو متعالٍ عن مجانسٍ؛ فلم يَصحَّ أن تكونَ له صاحبة، فلم تَصحَّ الولادة، قالَه في تفسيرِ هذه الآيةِ في الأنعام.","layers":[{"id":"K112.04","heading":"[K112.04 | T XVI 636; K IV 818]","text":"The meaning is: He is the God whom you know, and whom you acknowledge as the creator of the heavens and the earth and your own creator. He is one, alone in divinity, with no partner in it. He is the one to whom every creature turns; they cannot dispense with Him, whereas He has no need of them.\n“He did not beget,” because He shares no genus with anything, such that He might have a consort of His own genus and the two might produce offspring. His statement “How could He have a child, when He has no consort?” [6:101] indicates this meaning. “Nor was He begotten,” because everything begotten is originated and a body; He, however, is pre-eternal (qadīm (قديم)), with no beginning to His existence, and is not a body. No one equals Him: that is, no one resembles Him or is like Him. It is also possible to take this from suitability between spouses (kafāʾa (كفاءة)) in marriage, thereby denying Him a consort.","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":112,"ar":"والمعنى: هو الله الذي تعرفونه وتقرّون بأنه خالق السماوات والأرض وخالقكم، وهو واحد متوحد بالإلهية لا يشارك فيها، وهو الذي يصمد إليه كل مخلوق ولا يستغنون عنه، وهو الغنى عنهم. (لَمْ يَلِدْ) لأنه لا يجانس، حتى يكون له من جنسه صاحبة فيتوالدا. وقد دل على هذا المعنى بقوله: (أَنَّى يَكُونُ لَهُ وَلَدٌ وَلَمْ تَكُنْ لَهُ صاحِبَةٌ) [ الأنعام: 101 ]. (وَلَمْ يُولَدْ) لأنّ كل مولود محدث وجسم، وهو قديم لا أوّل لوجوده وليس بجسم ولم يكافئه أحد، أي: لم يماثله ولم يشاكله.\n\nويجوز أن يكون من الكفاءة في النكاح، نفيًا للصاحبة:","html":"<p>The meaning is: He is the God whom you know, and whom you acknowledge as the creator of the heavens and the earth and your own creator. He is one, alone in divinity, with no partner in it. He is the one to whom every creature turns; they cannot dispense with Him, whereas He has no need of them.\n“He did not beget,” because He shares no genus with anything, such that He might have a consort of His own genus and the two might produce offspring. His statement “How could He have a child, when He has no consort?” [6:101] indicates this meaning. “Nor was He begotten,” because everything begotten is originated and a body; He, however, is pre-eternal (qadīm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">قديم</bdi>)), with no beginning to His existence, and is not a body. No one equals Him: that is, no one resembles Him or is like Him. It is also possible to take this from suitability between spouses (kafāʾa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كفاءة</bdi>)) in marriage, thereby denying Him a consort.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true,"display_normalization":"Reversed electronic reference brackets normalized; source text preserved in the dossier."}},{"id":"T112.05","heading":"[T112.05 | XVI 636 | on K112.03–04]","text":"Al-Rāghib: “That which has no interior cavity is of two kinds: what is below the human being, such as inanimate things; and what is above, namely the Creator, exalted and holy. The purpose of al-Ṣamad is to indicate that He differs from those to whom they ascribed divinity. Something of this kind is indicated by ‘His mother was a woman of truth; they both used to eat food’ [5:75].”\nHis words, “His statement ‘How could He have a child, when He has no consort?’ indicates this meaning”: these are coordinated with “because He shares no genus.” That is, “He did not beget” either indirectly signifies (kināya (كناية)) His transcendence above belonging to a genus—for whoever shares a genus with anything takes a consort of his genus, and whoever takes a consort produces offspring—or [the reasoning proceeds] in reverse: how could He have a child when He has taken no consort? Generation occurs only between two partners of one genus. He transcends having a congener, so a consort cannot properly be ascribed to Him, and neither can generation. He states this in explaining the verse in al-Anʿām.","title":"“His statement ‘How could He have a child, when He has no consort?’ indicates this meaning”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.03","K112.04"],"anchor":"K112.04","surah":112,"ar":"الراغب: «الذي ليسَ بأجوفٍ، شيئان: أدونُ من الإنسانِ كالجمادات، وأعلى وهو الباري تعالى وتَقدّس. والقصدُ بقوله ﴿الصَّمَدُ﴾، تنبيهٌ أنه بخلافِ مَن أثبتوا له الإلهية، وإلى نحوِ هذا أشار بقولِه: ﴿وَأُمُّهُ صِدِّيقَةٌ كَانَا يَاكُلَانِ الطَّعَامَ﴾ [المائدة: 75]».\n\nقولُه: (وقَدْ دَلَّ على هذا المعنى بقوله: ﴿أَنَّى يَكُونُ لَهُ وَلَدٌ وَلَمْ تَكُنْ لَهُ صَاحِبَةٌ﴾ [الأنعام: 101]، عطفٌ على قوله: (لأنه لا يُجانَس)، يعني: ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ﴾: إما كنايةٌ عن كونه تعالى متعاليًا عن الجنسية؛ لأن مَن جانسَ شيئًا اتّخذ من جنسِه صاحبةً، ومَن اتخذَ صاحبةٍ حصَل التوالدُ.\n\nأو بالعكس بأن يقال: كيف يكونُ له ولدٌ، وأنه ما اتخذ صاحبةً؟ لأن الولادةَ لا تكونُ إلّا بين زوجين من جنسٍ واحد، وهو متعالٍ عن مجانسٍ؛ فلم يَصحَّ أن تكونَ له صاحبة، فلم تَصحَّ الولادة، قالَه في تفسيرِ هذه الآيةِ في الأنعام.","html":"<p>Al-Rāghib: “That which has no interior cavity is of two kinds: what is below the human being, such as inanimate things; and what is above, namely the Creator, exalted and holy. The purpose of al-Ṣamad is to indicate that He differs from those to whom they ascribed divinity. Something of this kind is indicated by ‘His mother was a woman of truth; they both used to eat food’ [5:75].”\nHis words, “His statement ‘How could He have a child, when He has no consort?’ indicates this meaning”: these are coordinated with “because He shares no genus.” That is, “He did not beget” either indirectly signifies (kināya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كناية</bdi>)) His transcendence above belonging to a genus—for whoever shares a genus with anything takes a consort of his genus, and whoever takes a consort produces offspring—or [the reasoning proceeds] in reverse: how could He have a child when He has taken no consort? Generation occurs only between two partners of one genus. He transcends having a congener, so a consort cannot properly be ascribed to Him, and neither can generation. He states this in explaining the verse in al-Anʿām.</p>","lemma":"وأمه صديقة كانا ياكلان الطعام","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"112:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ","en":"Say: He is Allah, the One"},{"key":"112:2","number":2,"ar":"ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ","en":"Allah, the eternally Besought of all"},{"key":"112:3","number":3,"ar":"لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ","en":"He begetteth not nor was begotten"},{"key":"112:4","number":4,"ar":"وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ","en":"And there is none comparable unto Him"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ ﴿1﴾ ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ ﴿2﴾ لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ ﴿3﴾ وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: He is Allah, the One 2. Allah, the eternally Besought of all 3. He begetteth not nor was begotten 4. And there is none comparable unto Him"},{"id":"K112.05","q":"112:1–4","s":112,"surah":"al-Ikhlāṣ","surah_ar":"الإخلاص","title":"al-Ikhlāṣ · They asked him to describe Him to them, and what encompasses His attributes was revealed to him","cairo":"K IV:817–819 · T XVI:632–643","source_page_first":817,"source_page_last":819,"chunk":"passages-112","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>They asked him to describe Him to them, and what encompasses His attributes was revealed to him. “He is God” points them to the creator and originator of things. Implicit in that is His description as powerful and knowing, since creation requires power and knowledge, occurring as it does with the utmost precision, coherence and order. This also describes Him as living, hearing and seeing. “One” describes His oneness and denies partners. “Al-Ṣamad” describes Him as exclusively the one who is needed; if He is exclusively needed [and not needy], He is self-sufficient. His being self-sufficient together with His being knowing entails that He is just and does not perform evil acts: He knows the evil of the evil act, and knows that He has no need of it. “Nor was He begotten” describes pre-eternity and primordiality. “He did not beget” denies likeness and a shared genus. “No one is equal to Him” confirms this and settles the judgment definitively.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K112.05"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“Thus His statement ‘He is God.’”","html":"<p>His words, “Thus His statement ‘He is God.’” The fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) introduces a detailed exposition; the general statement being expounded is “what encompasses His attributes.” Since Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) names the essence—and in the Opening of the Book he established the impossibility of its being a descriptive epithet—it nevertheless has in each context a meaning appropriate to that context. The particular character of the polytheists&#39; question required its explanation as “the Creator,” because of His statement, “If you ask them who created the heavens and the earth, they will certainly say: God” [31:25]. Thus Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) here, as an answer, points them to the one who creates things. You know that knowledge and power are what make creatorship possible: these two attributes are therefore included under it. This is what “implicit in that is His description as powerful and knowing” indicates. He cannot be powerful and knowing without being knowing, living, hearing and seeing. Then these descriptions were followed by the meaning of oneness in “One.” Since uniqueness excludes recourse to another, He was affirmed to possess ṣamadiyya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صمدية</bdi>), so that recourse would be to Him.\nOnce this established an essence entailing the attributes of creatorship, knowledge, power, life and divinity, their perfection was to be explained, together with their difference from creatures&#39; attributes in past and future. At this point it was said: “He neither begot nor was begotten, and no one is equal to Him.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.06"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Commentary","html":"<p>Ḥujjat al-Islām gives a concise discussion of them:\n“‘Aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>)’: He is the wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>) from whom partnership is excluded, and the aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) in whom there is no composition. Wāḥid negates partner and likeness; aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) negates multiplicity within His essence. Al-Ṣamad is the self-sufficient one whom others need. He is aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) in essence and wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>) in attributes: for if He had a partner in His dominion, He would not be self-sufficient and needed by others; rather, He would depend for His subsistence and existence on the components of His composition. Thus ṣamadiyya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صمدية</bdi>) proves both wāḥidiyya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحدية</bdi>) and aḥadiyya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحدية</bdi>).\n“‘He did not beget’ proves that His continuing existence is not like human existence, whose species persists through generation and reproduction; rather, His existence continues eternally, without beginning or end. ‘Nor was He begotten’ proves that His existence is not like the existence of the human soul, which comes into being after nonexistence and then remains forever, either in a lofty Garden that does not perish or in an abyss that does not end.\n“‘No one is equal to Him’ proves that His true existence is an existence that bestows existence on another without receiving existence from another. Thus ‘He is God, One’ proves His holy, transcendent essence. Ṣamadiyya entails His freedom from need and others&#39; need of Him. ‘Nor was He begotten,’ at the end of the sūrah, removes from Him what characterizes others. There is no clearer path to knowledge of God Most High than removing from Him the attributes of creatures.”\n[REVIEW T112-U1: The scan prints “nor was He begotten” in the last sentence, although it is not the sūrah&#39;s final clause. It is preserved. The wording about partnership, composition and attributes also needs comparison with the work being quoted.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.07"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"سألوه أن يصفه لهم، فأوحى إليه ما يحتوى على صفاته، فقوله: (هُوَ اللَّهُ) إشارة لهم إلى من هو خالق الأشياء وفاطرها،\n\nوفي طيّ ذلك وصفه بأنه قادر عالم؛ لأن الخلق يستدعى القدرة والعلم، لكونه واقعًا على غاية إحكام واتساق وانتظام، وفي ذلك وصفه بأنه حي سميع بصير. وقوله: (أَحَدٌ) وصف بالوحدانية ونفى الشركاء. وقوله: (الصَّمَدُ) وصف بأنه ليس إلا محتاجًا إليه، وإذا لم يكن إلا محتاجًا إليه، فهو غنى، وفي كونه غنيًا مع كونه عالمًا، أنه عدل غير فاعل للقبائح، لعلمه بقبح القبيح وعلمه بغناه عنه. وقوله: (ولَمْ يُولَدْ) وصف بالقدم والأوّلية. وقوله: (لَمْ يَلِدْ) نفى للشبه والمجانسة.\n\nوقوله: (وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ كُفُواً أَحَدٌ) تقرير لذلك وبت للحكم به.\n\nقولُه: (فقولُه: ﴿هُوَ اللَّهُ﴾)، الفاءُ تفصيليةٌ، والُمجمَلُ قولُه: \"ما يحتوي على صفاته\". ولمّا كان الله اسمًا للذات، وقرّرَ في فاتحةِ الكتابِ استحالةَ كونِه وصفًا، لكنْ له في كلِّ مقامٍ بحسبِ\n\nمقتضاه معنى، وخصوصيةُ سؤالِ المشركين، أوجبَ أن يفسّرَ بأنه الخالق، لقوله تعالى: ﴿وَلَئِنْ سَأَلْتَهُمْ مَنْ خَلَقَ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضَ لَيَقُولُنَّ اللَّهُ﴾ [لقمان: 25]؛ فاللهُ ها هنا، جوابًا، إشارةٌ لهم إلى مَن هو خالقُ الأشياء؟ وأنتَ تعلمُ أنّ مُصحّحَ الخالقية هو العلمُ والقُدرةُ، فاندرج تحته هاتانِ الصفتان، وإليه الإشارةُ بقولِه: \"وفي طيِّ ذلك وَصَفَه بأنه قادرٌ عالم\"، ولا يكونُ قادرًا عالماً، حتى يكونَ عالماً حيًّا سميعًا بصيرًا. ثُم عَقبَ هذه الأوصافَ معنى الوحدانية بقولِه: ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾.\n\nولما اقتضى الفردانيةُ قَطْعَ السبيلِ من الغير، أثبتَ له صفةَ الصَّمدانية، ليكون الإلتجاءُ إليه.\n\nولما عُلم من ذلك ثبوتُ الذاتِ المستلزمةِ للصفاتِ من الخالقيةِ والعالمِيةِ والقادريةِ والحييّةِ والإلهية، أريدَ بيانُ كمالهِا وأنها مباينةٌ لصفاتِ المخلوقاتِ فيما مضى ويُستقبل.\n\nوالآن قيل: ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ (3) وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌ (4)﴾،\n\nولحجةِ الإسلامِ كلامٌ إجماليٌ فيها، قال: «أحدٌ: هو الواحدُ الذي هو مرفوعُ الشركة، والأَحَدُ الذي لا تركيبَ فيه فالواحدُ نفيُ الشريكِ والمثلِ، والأحدُ نفيٌ للكثرةِ في ذاتِه، والصمدُ الغنيُّ المحتاجُ إليه غيرُه، وهو أحديُّ الذاتِ وواحديُّ الصفات، لأنه لو كان له شريكٌ في مُلكِه، لما كانَ غنيًا يحتاجُ إليه غيرُه، بل كانَ محتاجًا في قوامِه ووجودَه إلى أجزاءِ تركيبِه؛ فالصمديّةُ دليلٌ على الوحدانيةِ والأحديةِ، و ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ﴾ دليلٌ على أن وجوده المستمرّ، ليس مثل وجود الإنسانِ الذي يبقى نوعُه بالتوالدِ والتناسلِ، بل هو وجودٌ مستمرٌ أزليٌ أبديّ، و ﴿وَلَمْ يُولَدْ﴾ دليلٌ على أنّ وجودِه ليس مثلَ وجودِ نفس الإنسان الذي يتحصّلُ بعدَ العدم: يبقى دائمًا إمّا في جنةٍ عاليةٍ لا تفنى، وإمّا في هاويةٍ لا تنقطع.\n\n﴿وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌ﴾، دليلُ على الوجودِ الحقيقي الذي له تعالى، هو الوجودُ الذي يفيدُ وجودَ غيرِه، ولا يستفيدُ الوجودُ من غيرِه؛ فقوله تعالى: ﴿هُوَ اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ﴾، دليلٌ على إثباتِ ذاتِه المقدّسةِ المنزَّهة. والصَّمديةُ تقتضي نفي الحاجةِ عنه واحتياجَ غيرهِ إليه،\n\n﴿وَلَمْ يُولَدْ﴾ في آخر السورة، سلبُ ما يوصفُ به غيره عنه، ولا طريق في معرفة الله تعالى أوضحُ مِن سلبِ صفاتِ المخلوقات عنه».","layers":[{"id":"K112.05","heading":"[K112.05 | T XVI 636, 638; K IV 818]","text":"They asked him to describe Him to them, and what encompasses His attributes was revealed to him. “He is God” points them to the creator and originator of things. Implicit in that is His description as powerful and knowing, since creation requires power and knowledge, occurring as it does with the utmost precision, coherence and order. This also describes Him as living, hearing and seeing. “One” describes His oneness and denies partners. “Al-Ṣamad” describes Him as exclusively the one who is needed; if He is exclusively needed [and not needy], He is self-sufficient. His being self-sufficient together with His being knowing entails that He is just and does not perform evil acts: He knows the evil of the evil act, and knows that He has no need of it. “Nor was He begotten” describes pre-eternity and primordiality. “He did not beget” denies likeness and a shared genus. “No one is equal to Him” confirms this and settles the judgment definitively.","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":112,"ar":"سألوه أن يصفه لهم، فأوحى إليه ما يحتوى على صفاته، فقوله: (هُوَ اللَّهُ) إشارة لهم إلى من هو خالق الأشياء وفاطرها،\n\nوفي طيّ ذلك وصفه بأنه قادر عالم؛ لأن الخلق يستدعى القدرة والعلم، لكونه واقعًا على غاية إحكام واتساق وانتظام، وفي ذلك وصفه بأنه حي سميع بصير. وقوله: (أَحَدٌ) وصف بالوحدانية ونفى الشركاء. وقوله: (الصَّمَدُ) وصف بأنه ليس إلا محتاجًا إليه، وإذا لم يكن إلا محتاجًا إليه، فهو غنى، وفي كونه غنيًا مع كونه عالمًا، أنه عدل غير فاعل للقبائح، لعلمه بقبح القبيح وعلمه بغناه عنه. وقوله: (ولَمْ يُولَدْ) وصف بالقدم والأوّلية. وقوله: (لَمْ يَلِدْ) نفى للشبه والمجانسة.\n\nوقوله: (وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ كُفُواً أَحَدٌ) تقرير لذلك وبت للحكم به.","html":"<p>They asked him to describe Him to them, and what encompasses His attributes was revealed to him. “He is God” points them to the creator and originator of things. Implicit in that is His description as powerful and knowing, since creation requires power and knowledge, occurring as it does with the utmost precision, coherence and order. This also describes Him as living, hearing and seeing. “One” describes His oneness and denies partners. “Al-Ṣamad” describes Him as exclusively the one who is needed; if He is exclusively needed [and not needy], He is self-sufficient. His being self-sufficient together with His being knowing entails that He is just and does not perform evil acts: He knows the evil of the evil act, and knows that He has no need of it. “Nor was He begotten” describes pre-eternity and primordiality. “He did not beget” denies likeness and a shared genus. “No one is equal to Him” confirms this and settles the judgment definitively.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.06","heading":"[T112.06 | XVI 636–637 | on K112.05]","text":"His words, “Thus His statement ‘He is God.’” The fāʾ (ف) introduces a detailed exposition; the general statement being expounded is “what encompasses His attributes.” Since Allāh (الله) names the essence—and in the Opening of the Book he established the impossibility of its being a descriptive epithet—it nevertheless has in each context a meaning appropriate to that context. The particular character of the polytheists' question required its explanation as “the Creator,” because of His statement, “If you ask them who created the heavens and the earth, they will certainly say: God” [31:25]. Thus Allāh (الله) here, as an answer, points them to the one who creates things. You know that knowledge and power are what make creatorship possible: these two attributes are therefore included under it. This is what “implicit in that is His description as powerful and knowing” indicates. He cannot be powerful and knowing without being knowing, living, hearing and seeing. Then these descriptions were followed by the meaning of oneness in “One.” Since uniqueness excludes recourse to another, He was affirmed to possess ṣamadiyya (صمدية), so that recourse would be to Him.\nOnce this established an essence entailing the attributes of creatorship, knowledge, power, life and divinity, their perfection was to be explained, together with their difference from creatures' attributes in past and future. At this point it was said: “He neither begot nor was begotten, and no one is equal to Him.”","title":"“Thus His statement ‘He is God.’”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.05"],"anchor":"K112.05","surah":112,"ar":"قولُه: (فقولُه: ﴿هُوَ اللَّهُ﴾)، الفاءُ تفصيليةٌ، والُمجمَلُ قولُه: \"ما يحتوي على صفاته\". ولمّا كان الله اسمًا للذات، وقرّرَ في فاتحةِ الكتابِ استحالةَ كونِه وصفًا، لكنْ له في كلِّ مقامٍ بحسبِ\n\nمقتضاه معنى، وخصوصيةُ سؤالِ المشركين، أوجبَ أن يفسّرَ بأنه الخالق، لقوله تعالى: ﴿وَلَئِنْ سَأَلْتَهُمْ مَنْ خَلَقَ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضَ لَيَقُولُنَّ اللَّهُ﴾ [لقمان: 25]؛ فاللهُ ها هنا، جوابًا، إشارةٌ لهم إلى مَن هو خالقُ الأشياء؟ وأنتَ تعلمُ أنّ مُصحّحَ الخالقية هو العلمُ والقُدرةُ، فاندرج تحته هاتانِ الصفتان، وإليه الإشارةُ بقولِه: \"وفي طيِّ ذلك وَصَفَه بأنه قادرٌ عالم\"، ولا يكونُ قادرًا عالماً، حتى يكونَ عالماً حيًّا سميعًا بصيرًا. ثُم عَقبَ هذه الأوصافَ معنى الوحدانية بقولِه: ﴿أَحَدٌ﴾.\n\nولما اقتضى الفردانيةُ قَطْعَ السبيلِ من الغير، أثبتَ له صفةَ الصَّمدانية، ليكون الإلتجاءُ إليه.\n\nولما عُلم من ذلك ثبوتُ الذاتِ المستلزمةِ للصفاتِ من الخالقيةِ والعالمِيةِ والقادريةِ والحييّةِ والإلهية، أريدَ بيانُ كمالهِا وأنها مباينةٌ لصفاتِ المخلوقاتِ فيما مضى ويُستقبل.\n\nوالآن قيل: ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ (3) وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌ (4)﴾،","html":"<p>His words, “Thus His statement ‘He is God.’” The fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) introduces a detailed exposition; the general statement being expounded is “what encompasses His attributes.” Since Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) names the essence—and in the Opening of the Book he established the impossibility of its being a descriptive epithet—it nevertheless has in each context a meaning appropriate to that context. The particular character of the polytheists&#39; question required its explanation as “the Creator,” because of His statement, “If you ask them who created the heavens and the earth, they will certainly say: God” [31:25]. Thus Allāh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الله</bdi>) here, as an answer, points them to the one who creates things. You know that knowledge and power are what make creatorship possible: these two attributes are therefore included under it. This is what “implicit in that is His description as powerful and knowing” indicates. He cannot be powerful and knowing without being knowing, living, hearing and seeing. Then these descriptions were followed by the meaning of oneness in “One.” Since uniqueness excludes recourse to another, He was affirmed to possess ṣamadiyya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صمدية</bdi>), so that recourse would be to Him.\nOnce this established an essence entailing the attributes of creatorship, knowledge, power, life and divinity, their perfection was to be explained, together with their difference from creatures&#39; attributes in past and future. At this point it was said: “He neither begot nor was begotten, and no one is equal to Him.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.07","heading":"[T112.07 | XVI 637–638 | continuation]","text":"Ḥujjat al-Islām gives a concise discussion of them:\n“‘Aḥad (أحد)’: He is the wāḥid (واحد) from whom partnership is excluded, and the aḥad (أحد) in whom there is no composition. Wāḥid negates partner and likeness; aḥad (أحد) negates multiplicity within His essence. Al-Ṣamad is the self-sufficient one whom others need. He is aḥad (أحد) in essence and wāḥid (واحد) in attributes: for if He had a partner in His dominion, He would not be self-sufficient and needed by others; rather, He would depend for His subsistence and existence on the components of His composition. Thus ṣamadiyya (صمدية) proves both wāḥidiyya (واحدية) and aḥadiyya (أحدية).\n“‘He did not beget’ proves that His continuing existence is not like human existence, whose species persists through generation and reproduction; rather, His existence continues eternally, without beginning or end. ‘Nor was He begotten’ proves that His existence is not like the existence of the human soul, which comes into being after nonexistence and then remains forever, either in a lofty Garden that does not perish or in an abyss that does not end.\n“‘No one is equal to Him’ proves that His true existence is an existence that bestows existence on another without receiving existence from another. Thus ‘He is God, One’ proves His holy, transcendent essence. Ṣamadiyya entails His freedom from need and others' need of Him. ‘Nor was He begotten,’ at the end of the sūrah, removes from Him what characterizes others. There is no clearer path to knowledge of God Most High than removing from Him the attributes of creatures.”\n[REVIEW T112-U1: The scan prints “nor was He begotten” in the last sentence, although it is not the sūrah's final clause. It is preserved. The wording about partnership, composition and attributes also needs comparison with the work being quoted.]","title":"Commentary","author":"tibi","on":["K112.05"],"anchor":"K112.05","surah":112,"ar":"ولحجةِ الإسلامِ كلامٌ إجماليٌ فيها، قال: «أحدٌ: هو الواحدُ الذي هو مرفوعُ الشركة، والأَحَدُ الذي لا تركيبَ فيه فالواحدُ نفيُ الشريكِ والمثلِ، والأحدُ نفيٌ للكثرةِ في ذاتِه، والصمدُ الغنيُّ المحتاجُ إليه غيرُه، وهو أحديُّ الذاتِ وواحديُّ الصفات، لأنه لو كان له شريكٌ في مُلكِه، لما كانَ غنيًا يحتاجُ إليه غيرُه، بل كانَ محتاجًا في قوامِه ووجودَه إلى أجزاءِ تركيبِه؛ فالصمديّةُ دليلٌ على الوحدانيةِ والأحديةِ، و ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ﴾ دليلٌ على أن وجوده المستمرّ، ليس مثل وجود الإنسانِ الذي يبقى نوعُه بالتوالدِ والتناسلِ، بل هو وجودٌ مستمرٌ أزليٌ أبديّ، و ﴿وَلَمْ يُولَدْ﴾ دليلٌ على أنّ وجودِه ليس مثلَ وجودِ نفس الإنسان الذي يتحصّلُ بعدَ العدم: يبقى دائمًا إمّا في جنةٍ عاليةٍ لا تفنى، وإمّا في هاويةٍ لا تنقطع.\n\n﴿وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌ﴾، دليلُ على الوجودِ الحقيقي الذي له تعالى، هو الوجودُ الذي يفيدُ وجودَ غيرِه، ولا يستفيدُ الوجودُ من غيرِه؛ فقوله تعالى: ﴿هُوَ اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ﴾، دليلٌ على إثباتِ ذاتِه المقدّسةِ المنزَّهة. والصَّمديةُ تقتضي نفي الحاجةِ عنه واحتياجَ غيرهِ إليه،\n\n﴿وَلَمْ يُولَدْ﴾ في آخر السورة، سلبُ ما يوصفُ به غيره عنه، ولا طريق في معرفة الله تعالى أوضحُ مِن سلبِ صفاتِ المخلوقات عنه».","html":"<p>Ḥujjat al-Islām gives a concise discussion of them:\n“‘Aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>)’: He is the wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>) from whom partnership is excluded, and the aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) in whom there is no composition. Wāḥid negates partner and likeness; aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) negates multiplicity within His essence. Al-Ṣamad is the self-sufficient one whom others need. He is aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) in essence and wāḥid (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحد</bdi>) in attributes: for if He had a partner in His dominion, He would not be self-sufficient and needed by others; rather, He would depend for His subsistence and existence on the components of His composition. Thus ṣamadiyya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صمدية</bdi>) proves both wāḥidiyya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">واحدية</bdi>) and aḥadiyya (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحدية</bdi>).\n“‘He did not beget’ proves that His continuing existence is not like human existence, whose species persists through generation and reproduction; rather, His existence continues eternally, without beginning or end. ‘Nor was He begotten’ proves that His existence is not like the existence of the human soul, which comes into being after nonexistence and then remains forever, either in a lofty Garden that does not perish or in an abyss that does not end.\n“‘No one is equal to Him’ proves that His true existence is an existence that bestows existence on another without receiving existence from another. Thus ‘He is God, One’ proves His holy, transcendent essence. Ṣamadiyya entails His freedom from need and others&#39; need of Him. ‘Nor was He begotten,’ at the end of the sūrah, removes from Him what characterizes others. There is no clearer path to knowledge of God Most High than removing from Him the attributes of creatures.”\n[REVIEW T112-U1: The scan prints “nor was He begotten” in the last sentence, although it is not the sūrah&#39;s final clause. It is preserved. The wording about partnership, composition and attributes also needs comparison with the work being quoted.]</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"112:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ","en":"Say: He is Allah, the One"},{"key":"112:2","number":2,"ar":"ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ","en":"Allah, the eternally Besought of all"},{"key":"112:3","number":3,"ar":"لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ","en":"He begetteth not nor was begotten"},{"key":"112:4","number":4,"ar":"وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ","en":"And there is none comparable unto Him"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ ﴿1﴾ ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ ﴿2﴾ لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ ﴿3﴾ وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: He is Allah, the One 2. Allah, the eternally Besought of all 3. He begetteth not nor was begotten 4. And there is none comparable unto Him"},{"id":"K112.06","q":"112:4","s":112,"surah":"al-Ikhlāṣ","surah_ar":"الإخلاص","title":"al-Ikhlāṣ · If you ask, “In eloquent Arabic, an adjunct (ẓarf) that is supplementary (laghw), rather than indisp","cairo":"K IV:817–819 · T XVI:632–643","source_page_first":817,"source_page_last":819,"chunk":"passages-112","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>If you ask, “In eloquent Arabic, an adjunct (ẓarf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ظرف</bdi>)) that is supplementary (laghw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لغو</bdi>)), rather than indispensable to the predication (mustaqirr (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مستقرّ</bdi>)), is postponed, not placed first—Sībawayh explicitly says so in his book. Why, then, is it placed first in the most eloquent and most fully Arabic of speech?”, I answer: this utterance is directed precisely at denying equivalence to the essence of the Creator—glory be to Him. This adjunct [lahu, ‘to Him’] is where that meaning converges and is centred. It is therefore the most important and most pertinent element, the one most entitled and most suited to precedence.\nKufuʾan has been read with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) on both kāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ك</bdi>) and fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>), and also with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) or kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) on the kāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ك</bdi>) together with an unvocalized fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) [kufʾan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كُفْئًا</bdi>), kifʾan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كِفْئًا</bdi>)].</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K112.06"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“Exclusively the one who is needed”","html":"<p>His words, “Exclusively the one who is needed”: the exception is unfilled (istithnāʾ mufarragh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">استثناء مفرّغ</bdi>)); that is, God is exclusively the one who is needed—relative, that is, to creatures.\nHis words, “Supplementary, not indispensable to the predication.” An indispensable adjunct (ẓarf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ظرف</bdi>) mustaqirr (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مستقرّ</bdi>)) is one required to complete the utterance, as when it functions as the predicate in “There was not in it anyone better than you.” A supplementary adjunct (laghw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لغو</bdi>)) is one without which the utterance is complete, as in “No one was better than you in it.” In the first case, the indispensable adjunct is put first because it is intended as the focus. The normal arrangement was abandoned in the verse because it was directed at explaining divine oneness.\nIbn al-Ḥājib says: “It was placed first out of concern for the correspondence of verse endings. Had it merely preceded aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>), the aim would have been achieved; but it would then have separated the two parts which are [the predicate and] the subject of predication. It was therefore placed before both, and the aim was achieved.”\n[REVIEW T112-U2: The printed wording is <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">اللذين هما مسند إليه</bdi>, apparently incomplete as a description of the two parts. The bracketed supplement conveys the apparent argument but requires checking the quoted source.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.08"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Commentary","html":"<p>The author of al-Intiṣāf says: “Sībawayh reports hearing a rough Arab recite ‘wa-lam yakun aḥadun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحدٌ</bdi>) kufuwan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كُفُوًا</bdi>) lahu.’ The uncouth man followed his habitual manner, his coarse disposition missing the subtle meaning that required the adjunct and predicate to precede the subject. The verse&#39;s intended aim is to deny equivalence and equality to God&#39;s essence. It was therefore preferable to put first the equivalence intended to be denied Him. Once it was brought forward for negation, the adjunct was mentioned with it, to distinguish the holy essence by the denial of equivalence.”\nI say: its upshot is that attention to the meaning demanded by the context is more fitting, more justified and prior to attention to wording and verse endings.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.09"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“Kufuʾan has been read, with ḍamma on kāf”","html":"<p>His words, “Kufuʾan has been read, with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) on kāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ك</bdi>).” Ḥafṣ reads with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) on it and on fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>), without hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) [kufuwan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كُفُوًا</bdi>)]. Ḥamza reads with unvocalized fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) and hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) when continuing; when stopping, he replaces it with a wāw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">و</bdi>) bearing fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>). The others read with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) on fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) and with hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>).\nAl-Rāghib: “Kufʾ is [equality] in standing and worth. From it comes kifāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كفاء</bdi>) for a piece of fabric sewn to another, with which the rear of a tent is covered. One says that a person is another&#39;s equal in marriage, combat and the like. From it comes mukāfaʾa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مكافأة</bdi>): equality and corresponding action. Ikfāʾ is overturning something, as though removing equality; from this comes ikfāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إكفاء</bdi>) in poetry.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.10"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"فإن قلت: الكلام العربي الفصيح أن يؤخر الظرف الذي هو لغو غير مستقر ولا يقدم، وقد نص سيبويه على ذلك في كتابه، فما باله مقدّمًا في أفصح كلام وأعربه؟\n\nقلت: هذا الكلام إنما سيق لنفى المكافأة عن ذات الباري سبحانه؛ وهذا المعنى مصبه ومركزه هو هذا الظرف، فكان لذلك أهم شيٍء وأعناه، وأحقه بالتقدم وأحراه. وقرئ: (كفؤا) بضم الكاف والفاء، وبضم الكاف وكسرها مع سكون الفاء.\n\nقولُه: (ليس إلا محتاجًا إليه)، والاستثناءُ مفرّغٌ، أي: ليس الله إلا محتاجًا إليه، أي بالنسبة إلى المخلوقات.\n\nقولُه: (لَغوٌ غير مستقر)، الظرفُ المستقر: هو الذي يفتقرُ تمام الكلامِ إليه، وذلك بأن يكون خبرًا كما في قولك: ما كانَ فيها أحدٌ خيرٌ منك. والَّلغوُ أن يكونَ الكلامُ تامًا بدونه كما في قولك: ما كانَ أحدٌ خيرًا منك فيها؛ وإنما قُدّم في اول المستقرّ لكونه مقصودًا، وإنما رُفِضَ في الآية الأصل، لأنها سبقت لبيانِ التوحيد. قالَ ابنُ الحاجب: «إنما قُدِّمَ لاهتمامِ تناسبِ الفواصل، فلو قُدِّمَ على \" أحد\" لحصلَ الغرض، لكن كان يقع الفصلُ بين الجزأين اللذين هما مسندٌ إليه، فقدَّمَ عليهما جميعً وحصلَ الغرض».\n\nوقال صاحبُ \"الانتصاف\": «نقلَ سيبويه أن سمعَ بعضَ الجُفاةِ من العربِ يقرأ: ولم يكنْ أحدٌ كفوًا له، فجرى هذا الجلفُ على عادته، فجفا طبعُه عن لُطفِ المعنى، الذي لأجله اقتضى تقديمَ الظرفِ والخبرِ على الاسم، وذلك أن الغرضَ الذي سيقت إليه الآيةُ، نفيُ المكافأة والمساواةِ عن ذاتِ الله تعالى، فكان تقديم المكافأةِ المقصودةِ بأن تُسلبَ عنه أنه أولى، ثم لما قُدِّمتْ لتسلبَ ذُكرَ معها الظرف، لتُبيَّنَ الذاتُ المقدسةُ بسلبِ المكافأة».\n\nوقلتُ: تلخيصُه أن مراعاةَ المعنى الذي يقتضيه المقامُ، أحرى وأحقُّ وأقدمُ من مراعاةِ اللفظِ والفواصلِ.\n\nقولُه: (وقرئ: ﴿كُفُوًا﴾، بضم الكاف)، حَفْص: بضِّمها وضِّم الفاء من غيرِ همز، وحمزة: بإسكانِ الفاءِ مع الهمزةِ في الوصل، فإذا وقفَ أبدلَ واوًا مفتوحة، والباقون: بضمِّ الفاءِ مع الهمزة.\n\nالراغب: «الكُفْءُ: في المنزلةِ والقَدْر، ومنه الكِفاءُ لشُقّةٍ تُنْصحُ بالأخرى، فيُجَلّلُ بها مؤخرُ الخباء. قال: فلانٌ كفءُ فلانٍ في المناكحةِ والمحاربةِ ونحوِ ذلك. ومنه المكافأةُ أي: المساواةُ والمقابلةُ في الفعل، والإكفاءُ: قلبُ الشئِ كأنه إزالةُ المساواة، ومنه الإكفاءُ في الشعر».","layers":[{"id":"K112.06","heading":"[K112.06 | T XVI 638–639; K IV 818–819]","text":"If you ask, “In eloquent Arabic, an adjunct (ẓarf (ظرف)) that is supplementary (laghw (لغو)), rather than indispensable to the predication (mustaqirr (مستقرّ)), is postponed, not placed first—Sībawayh explicitly says so in his book. Why, then, is it placed first in the most eloquent and most fully Arabic of speech?”, I answer: this utterance is directed precisely at denying equivalence to the essence of the Creator—glory be to Him. This adjunct [lahu, ‘to Him’] is where that meaning converges and is centred. It is therefore the most important and most pertinent element, the one most entitled and most suited to precedence.\nKufuʾan has been read with ḍamma (ضَمّة) on both kāf (ك) and fāʾ (ف), and also with ḍamma (ضَمّة) or kasra (كَسْرة) on the kāf (ك) together with an unvocalized fāʾ (ف) [kufʾan (كُفْئًا), kifʾan (كِفْئًا)].","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":112,"ar":"فإن قلت: الكلام العربي الفصيح أن يؤخر الظرف الذي هو لغو غير مستقر ولا يقدم، وقد نص سيبويه على ذلك في كتابه، فما باله مقدّمًا في أفصح كلام وأعربه؟\n\nقلت: هذا الكلام إنما سيق لنفى المكافأة عن ذات الباري سبحانه؛ وهذا المعنى مصبه ومركزه هو هذا الظرف، فكان لذلك أهم شيٍء وأعناه، وأحقه بالتقدم وأحراه. وقرئ: (كفؤا) بضم الكاف والفاء، وبضم الكاف وكسرها مع سكون الفاء.","html":"<p>If you ask, “In eloquent Arabic, an adjunct (ẓarf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ظرف</bdi>)) that is supplementary (laghw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لغو</bdi>)), rather than indispensable to the predication (mustaqirr (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مستقرّ</bdi>)), is postponed, not placed first—Sībawayh explicitly says so in his book. Why, then, is it placed first in the most eloquent and most fully Arabic of speech?”, I answer: this utterance is directed precisely at denying equivalence to the essence of the Creator—glory be to Him. This adjunct [lahu, ‘to Him’] is where that meaning converges and is centred. It is therefore the most important and most pertinent element, the one most entitled and most suited to precedence.\nKufuʾan has been read with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) on both kāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ك</bdi>) and fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>), and also with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) or kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) on the kāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ك</bdi>) together with an unvocalized fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) [kufʾan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كُفْئًا</bdi>), kifʾan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كِفْئًا</bdi>)].</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.08","heading":"[T112.08 | XVI 638 | on K112.05–06]","text":"His words, “Exclusively the one who is needed”: the exception is unfilled (istithnāʾ mufarragh (استثناء مفرّغ)); that is, God is exclusively the one who is needed—relative, that is, to creatures.\nHis words, “Supplementary, not indispensable to the predication.” An indispensable adjunct (ẓarf (ظرف) mustaqirr (مستقرّ)) is one required to complete the utterance, as when it functions as the predicate in “There was not in it anyone better than you.” A supplementary adjunct (laghw (لغو)) is one without which the utterance is complete, as in “No one was better than you in it.” In the first case, the indispensable adjunct is put first because it is intended as the focus. The normal arrangement was abandoned in the verse because it was directed at explaining divine oneness.\nIbn al-Ḥājib says: “It was placed first out of concern for the correspondence of verse endings. Had it merely preceded aḥad (أحد), the aim would have been achieved; but it would then have separated the two parts which are [the predicate and] the subject of predication. It was therefore placed before both, and the aim was achieved.”\n[REVIEW T112-U2: The printed wording is اللذين هما مسند إليه, apparently incomplete as a description of the two parts. The bracketed supplement conveys the apparent argument but requires checking the quoted source.]","title":"“Exclusively the one who is needed”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.05","K112.06"],"anchor":"K112.06","surah":112,"ar":"قولُه: (ليس إلا محتاجًا إليه)، والاستثناءُ مفرّغٌ، أي: ليس الله إلا محتاجًا إليه، أي بالنسبة إلى المخلوقات.\n\nقولُه: (لَغوٌ غير مستقر)، الظرفُ المستقر: هو الذي يفتقرُ تمام الكلامِ إليه، وذلك بأن يكون خبرًا كما في قولك: ما كانَ فيها أحدٌ خيرٌ منك. والَّلغوُ أن يكونَ الكلامُ تامًا بدونه كما في قولك: ما كانَ أحدٌ خيرًا منك فيها؛ وإنما قُدّم في اول المستقرّ لكونه مقصودًا، وإنما رُفِضَ في الآية الأصل، لأنها سبقت لبيانِ التوحيد. قالَ ابنُ الحاجب: «إنما قُدِّمَ لاهتمامِ تناسبِ الفواصل، فلو قُدِّمَ على \" أحد\" لحصلَ الغرض، لكن كان يقع الفصلُ بين الجزأين اللذين هما مسندٌ إليه، فقدَّمَ عليهما جميعً وحصلَ الغرض».","html":"<p>His words, “Exclusively the one who is needed”: the exception is unfilled (istithnāʾ mufarragh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">استثناء مفرّغ</bdi>)); that is, God is exclusively the one who is needed—relative, that is, to creatures.\nHis words, “Supplementary, not indispensable to the predication.” An indispensable adjunct (ẓarf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ظرف</bdi>) mustaqirr (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مستقرّ</bdi>)) is one required to complete the utterance, as when it functions as the predicate in “There was not in it anyone better than you.” A supplementary adjunct (laghw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لغو</bdi>)) is one without which the utterance is complete, as in “No one was better than you in it.” In the first case, the indispensable adjunct is put first because it is intended as the focus. The normal arrangement was abandoned in the verse because it was directed at explaining divine oneness.\nIbn al-Ḥājib says: “It was placed first out of concern for the correspondence of verse endings. Had it merely preceded aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>), the aim would have been achieved; but it would then have separated the two parts which are [the predicate and] the subject of predication. It was therefore placed before both, and the aim was achieved.”\n[REVIEW T112-U2: The printed wording is <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">اللذين هما مسند إليه</bdi>, apparently incomplete as a description of the two parts. The bracketed supplement conveys the apparent argument but requires checking the quoted source.]</p>","lemma":"ليس إلا محتاجا إليه","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.09","heading":"[T112.09 | XVI 639 | on K112.06]","text":"The author of al-Intiṣāf says: “Sībawayh reports hearing a rough Arab recite ‘wa-lam yakun aḥadun (أحدٌ) kufuwan (كُفُوًا) lahu.’ The uncouth man followed his habitual manner, his coarse disposition missing the subtle meaning that required the adjunct and predicate to precede the subject. The verse's intended aim is to deny equivalence and equality to God's essence. It was therefore preferable to put first the equivalence intended to be denied Him. Once it was brought forward for negation, the adjunct was mentioned with it, to distinguish the holy essence by the denial of equivalence.”\nI say: its upshot is that attention to the meaning demanded by the context is more fitting, more justified and prior to attention to wording and verse endings.","title":"Commentary","author":"tibi","on":["K112.06"],"anchor":"K112.06","surah":112,"ar":"وقال صاحبُ \"الانتصاف\": «نقلَ سيبويه أن سمعَ بعضَ الجُفاةِ من العربِ يقرأ: ولم يكنْ أحدٌ كفوًا له، فجرى هذا الجلفُ على عادته، فجفا طبعُه عن لُطفِ المعنى، الذي لأجله اقتضى تقديمَ الظرفِ والخبرِ على الاسم، وذلك أن الغرضَ الذي سيقت إليه الآيةُ، نفيُ المكافأة والمساواةِ عن ذاتِ الله تعالى، فكان تقديم المكافأةِ المقصودةِ بأن تُسلبَ عنه أنه أولى، ثم لما قُدِّمتْ لتسلبَ ذُكرَ معها الظرف، لتُبيَّنَ الذاتُ المقدسةُ بسلبِ المكافأة».\n\nوقلتُ: تلخيصُه أن مراعاةَ المعنى الذي يقتضيه المقامُ، أحرى وأحقُّ وأقدمُ من مراعاةِ اللفظِ والفواصلِ.","html":"<p>The author of al-Intiṣāf says: “Sībawayh reports hearing a rough Arab recite ‘wa-lam yakun aḥadun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحدٌ</bdi>) kufuwan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كُفُوًا</bdi>) lahu.’ The uncouth man followed his habitual manner, his coarse disposition missing the subtle meaning that required the adjunct and predicate to precede the subject. The verse&#39;s intended aim is to deny equivalence and equality to God&#39;s essence. It was therefore preferable to put first the equivalence intended to be denied Him. Once it was brought forward for negation, the adjunct was mentioned with it, to distinguish the holy essence by the denial of equivalence.”\nI say: its upshot is that attention to the meaning demanded by the context is more fitting, more justified and prior to attention to wording and verse endings.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.10","heading":"[T112.10 | XVI 639 | on K112.06]","text":"His words, “Kufuʾan has been read, with ḍamma (ضَمّة) on kāf (ك).” Ḥafṣ reads with ḍamma (ضَمّة) on it and on fāʾ (ف), without hamza (همزة) [kufuwan (كُفُوًا)]. Ḥamza reads with unvocalized fāʾ (ف) and hamza (همزة) when continuing; when stopping, he replaces it with a wāw (و) bearing fatḥa (فَتْحة). The others read with ḍamma (ضَمّة) on fāʾ (ف) and with hamza (همزة).\nAl-Rāghib: “Kufʾ is [equality] in standing and worth. From it comes kifāʾ (كفاء) for a piece of fabric sewn to another, with which the rear of a tent is covered. One says that a person is another's equal in marriage, combat and the like. From it comes mukāfaʾa (مكافأة): equality and corresponding action. Ikfāʾ is overturning something, as though removing equality; from this comes ikfāʾ (إكفاء) in poetry.”","title":"“Kufuʾan has been read, with ḍamma on kāf”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.06"],"anchor":"K112.06","surah":112,"ar":"قولُه: (وقرئ: ﴿كُفُوًا﴾، بضم الكاف)، حَفْص: بضِّمها وضِّم الفاء من غيرِ همز، وحمزة: بإسكانِ الفاءِ مع الهمزةِ في الوصل، فإذا وقفَ أبدلَ واوًا مفتوحة، والباقون: بضمِّ الفاءِ مع الهمزة.\n\nالراغب: «الكُفْءُ: في المنزلةِ والقَدْر، ومنه الكِفاءُ لشُقّةٍ تُنْصحُ بالأخرى، فيُجَلّلُ بها مؤخرُ الخباء. قال: فلانٌ كفءُ فلانٍ في المناكحةِ والمحاربةِ ونحوِ ذلك. ومنه المكافأةُ أي: المساواةُ والمقابلةُ في الفعل، والإكفاءُ: قلبُ الشئِ كأنه إزالةُ المساواة، ومنه الإكفاءُ في الشعر».","html":"<p>His words, “Kufuʾan has been read, with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) on kāf (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ك</bdi>).” Ḥafṣ reads with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) on it and on fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>), without hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) [kufuwan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كُفُوًا</bdi>)]. Ḥamza reads with unvocalized fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) and hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) when continuing; when stopping, he replaces it with a wāw (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">و</bdi>) bearing fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>). The others read with ḍamma (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضَمّة</bdi>) on fāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ف</bdi>) and with hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>).\nAl-Rāghib: “Kufʾ is [equality] in standing and worth. From it comes kifāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كفاء</bdi>) for a piece of fabric sewn to another, with which the rear of a tent is covered. One says that a person is another&#39;s equal in marriage, combat and the like. From it comes mukāfaʾa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مكافأة</bdi>): equality and corresponding action. Ikfāʾ is overturning something, as though removing equality; from this comes ikfāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إكفاء</bdi>) in poetry.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"112:4","number":4,"ar":"وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ","en":"And there is none comparable unto Him"}],"quran_ar":"وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"4. And there is none comparable unto Him"},{"id":"K112.07","q":"112:1–4","s":112,"surah":"al-Ikhlāṣ","surah_ar":"الإخلاص","title":"al-Ikhlāṣ · If you ask, “Why is this sūrah the equivalent of the whole Qurʾān, despite its shortness and the clo","cairo":"K IV:817–819 · T XVI:632–643","source_page_first":817,"source_page_last":819,"chunk":"passages-112","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>If you ask, “Why is this sūrah the equivalent of the whole Qurʾān, despite its shortness and the closeness of its two ends?”, I answer:\n“It is for some great cause that whoever becomes a master becomes one.”\nThat is solely because it encompasses God&#39;s attributes, His justice and His oneness. For anyone who acknowledges its excellence and believes the Messenger of God&#39;s ﷺ statement about it, this is sufficient proof that the science of divine oneness (ʿilm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">علم</bdi>) al-tawḥīd) has an exalted standing with God. How could it be otherwise? Knowledge follows its object: it is ennobled by the nobility of what is known, and lowered by its lowliness. The object of this science is God Most High and His attributes, and what is and is not admissible concerning Him. What, then, do you think of the nobility of its rank, the grandeur of its position, its eminence over every other science, and its winning the prize ahead of them? Whoever disparages it does so because of the weakness of his knowledge of its object, the smallness of his reverence for Him, his lack of fear of Him, and his remoteness from considering his own ultimate fate.\nO God, gather us among those who know You, act for You, affirm Your justice and Your oneness, and fear Your threat.\nIt is called “The Sūrah of the Foundation,” because it encompasses the foundations of religion. Ubayy and Anas transmitted from the Prophet ﷺ: “The seven heavens and the seven earths were founded upon ‘Say: He is God, One.’” That is: they were created solely to serve as signs of God&#39;s oneness and of the knowledge of His attributes expressed by this sūrah.\nIt is related from the Messenger of God ﷺ that he heard a man reciting “Say: He is God, One,” and said, “It has become assured.” He was asked, “Messenger of God, what has become assured?” He answered, “Paradise has become assured for him.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K112.07"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“The equivalent of the whole Qurʾān”","html":"<p>His words, “The equivalent of the whole Qurʾān.” It is transmitted with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) or kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) on the ʿayn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ع</bdi>). Al-Akhfash says: “ʿIdl, with kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>), means an equivalent. ʿAdl, with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>), is originally the verbal noun in ‘I made this nicely equal [to that]’; you turn it into a noun for an equivalent to distinguish it from a ʿidl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عِدْل</bdi>), a load of goods.” Al-Farrāʾ says: “ʿAdl, with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>), is what equals something while belonging to a different kind. ʿIdl, with kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>), is its like. You say, ‘I have the ʿidl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عِدْل</bdi>) of your servant-boy, or of your sheep,’ when it is a servant-boy equal to a servant-boy or a sheep equal to a sheep. If you mean its value in something of another kind, you pronounce the ʿayn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ع</bdi>) with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>). Some Arabs sometimes pronounce it with kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>); that was an error on their part.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.11"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“It is for some cause that whoever becomes a master becomes one”","html":"<p>The correct version is “one third of the Qurʾān.” We have transmitted from al-Bukhārī, Muslim, Mālik, Abū Dāwūd and al-Nasāʾī, from Abū Saʿīd, that a man heard another reciting “Say: He is God, One” repeatedly. In the morning he came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned it, apparently regarding it as little. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, it is indeed equivalent to one third of the Qurʾān.”\nAl-Qāḍī says: “Because this sūrah, short as it is, encompasses all knowledge of divinity and refutes those who deviate concerning it, the report says that it equals one third of the Qurʾān. The Qurʾān&#39;s aims are confined to explaining beliefs, rulings and narratives. Whoever equates it with the whole takes into account what is intended for its own sake among these.”\nHis words, “It is for some cause that whoever becomes a master becomes one”: the opening is:\n“I resolved to stay until morning.”\nMā is an additional particle giving indefiniteness and magnification: that is, it is for a great cause that whoever becomes a master becomes one.\n[SOURCE CHECK T112-U3: Al-Baghdādī, Khizānat al-adab III:86–88, records the full line and explains <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إقامة ذي صباح</bdi> as remaining until morning. The rendering follows that explanation; it does not establish every detail of the poem&#39;s attribution or context.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.12"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“It suffices as proof for whoever acknowledges”","html":"<p>His words, “It suffices as proof for whoever acknowledges.” Man iʿtarafa [whoever acknowledges] is the object of kafā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كفى</bdi>) [suffices]. Its subject is what is indicated by “because it encompasses God&#39;s attributes.” The pronoun in “its excellence” refers to the sūrah. “Believes” is coordinated with “acknowledges”; “the Messenger of God&#39;s ﷺ statement” attaches to “believes.” “That the science of divine oneness” attaches to “as proof,” which is a specification (tamyīz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تمييز</bdi>)). That is: for whoever acknowledges the sūrah&#39;s excellence and believes the Messenger&#39;s statement, this suffices as proof that the science of divine oneness has an exalted standing with God.\nThe Prophet&#39;s ﷺ statement intended here is the one he transmits at the end of the sūrah: “The seven heavens were founded …,” to the end. I have not found this report in the recognized source collections.\nAl-Tirmidhī, Abū Dāwūd and Ibn Māja transmit from Burayda that the Messenger of God ﷺ heard a man saying: “O God, I ask You by my testimony that You are God—there is no god but You—the One, al-Ṣamad, who neither begot nor was begotten, and to whom no one is equal.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, he has asked God by His greatest name: when invoked by it, He answers; when asked by it, He gives.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.13"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“He said: It has become assured”","html":"<p>His words, “He said: It has become assured.” Mālik, Aḥmad, al-Tirmidhī and al-Nasāʾī transmitted the report from Abū Hurayra.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.14"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Commentary","html":"<p>A conclusion from the words of Shaykh Faṣīḥ al-Dīn—may God have mercy on him:\n“‘God, al-Ṣamad’ is not joined by a conjunction to the preceding sentence, because it confirms and explains its content. The same applies to ‘He did not beget,’ because it confirms the content of ‘God, al-Ṣamad’: the absolutely self-sufficient one, whom everything needs, cannot be a begetter or begotten, since that necessarily entails need.\n“‘Nor was He begotten’ is joined to ‘He did not beget,’ because ‘was not begotten’ does not express the meaning of ‘did not beget,’ and thus does not confirm its meaning; rather, the two sentences confirm the content of the preceding sentence. ‘And no one is equal to Him’ is joined [by a conjunction] because its content does not confirm the content of the two preceding sentences: it expresses that He cannot have any equal in anything mentioned concerning essence and attributes. He is therefore one, with no partner—exalted, holy and supremely great.\n“The predicate is definite in ‘God, al-Ṣamad’ to negate [the claim of] anyone who asserts and names another as ṣamad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صمد</bdi>). It is indefinite in ‘God, One,’ because they did not call things aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) in this sense.”\n[REVIEW T112-U4: The scan reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">نفيا لنفي من زعم وسمى غيره صمدا</bdi>, literally “to negate the negation of whoever asserted and named another ṣamad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صمد</bdi>).” This double negation is problematic. The bracketed rendering gives the apparent intended argument, not a verified emendation. Retain the flag pending another witness or the quoted source.]\nThe sūrah is complete.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T112.15"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"فإن قلت: لم كانت هذه السورة عدل القرآن كله على قصر منها وتقارب طرفيها؟ قلت:\n\nلأمر ما يسود من يسود\n\nوما ذاك إلا لاحتوائها على صفات الله تعالى وعدله وتوحيده، وكفى دليلًا من اعترف بفضلها وصدق بقول رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فيها: إنّ علم التوحيد من الله تعالى بمكان، وكيف لا يكون كذلك والعلم تابع للمعلوم: يشرف بشرفه، ويتضع بضعته؛ ومعلوم هذا العلم هو الله تعالى وصفاته، وما يجوز عليه وما لا يجوز، فما ظنك بشرف منزلته وجلالة محله،\n\nوإنافته على كل علم، واستيلائه على قصب السبق دونه؛ ومن ازدراه فلضعف علمه بمعلومة، وقلة تعظيمه له، وخلوه من خشيته، وبعده من النظر لعاقبته. اللهم احشرنا في زمرة العالمين بك العاملين لك، القائلين بعدلك وتوحيدك، الخائفين من وعيدك.\n\nوتسمى \"سورة الأساس\" لاشتمالها على أصول الدين، وروى أبىّ وأنس عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «أسست السماوات السبع والأرضون السبع على قل هو الله أحد»، يعنى ما خلقت إلا لتكون دلائل على توحيد الله ومعرفة صفاته التي نطقت بها هذه السورة.\n\nعن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه سمع رجلا يقرأ: (قل هو الله أحد)، فقال: «وجبت». قيل: يا رسول الله وما \"وجبت\"؟ قال: «وجبت له الجنة».\n\nقولُه: (عَدْلَ القرآنِ كلِّه)، يُروى بفتح العين وكسرها، قال الأخفش: العِدْلُ بالكسر: المِثْل، والعَدْلُ بالفتح: أصلُه مصدر قولك: عَدَلْتَ بهذا عَدْلًا حسنًا، تجعله اسمًا للمِثْلِ، لِتُفْرُق بينه وبين عِدْل المتاع. وقال الفرّاء: العَدْلُ بالفتح: ما عادلَ الشّئ من غير جنسه، والعِدْلُ بالكسر: المِثْل. وتقول: عندي عِدْلُ غلامك، وعِدْلُ شاتك، إذا كان غلامًا يعدلُ غلامًا، أو شاةً تعدلُ شاة، فإذا أردت قيمته من غير جنسه، نَصبتَ العين، وربّما كَسَرها بعضُ العرب، وكان منهم غلط.\n\nوالصحيحُ: ثلثُ القرآن؛ رَوينا عن البخاريّ ومسلم ومالك وأبي داودَ والنسائي، عن أبي سعيد، أن رجلًا سمعَ رجلًا يقرأ: ﴿قُلْ هُوَ اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ﴾ يردّدها، فلما أصبحَ جاءَ إلى النبيّ صلى الله عليه وسلم، فذكرَ ذلك له، وكأنّ الرجلَ يتقالُّها، فقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «والذي نفسي بيده، إنها لتعدلُ ثُلُثَ القرآن».\n\nقال القاضي: «ولاشتمال هذه السورة مع قِصرِها على جميع المعارفِ الإلهية، والردِّ على مَن ألحدَ فيها، جاء في الحديثِ أنها تعدلُ ثلثَ القرآن، لأن مقاصدِ القرآنِ محصورةٌ في بيانِ العقائد، والأحكام، والقصص، ومَن عَدَلَها بكلِّه اعتبرَ المقصودِ بالذاتِ من ذلك».\n\nقولُه: (لأمرٍ ما يُسَوَّدُ مَن يَسودُ)، أوله:\n\nعَزَمتُ على إقامةِ ذي صباحِ\n\nو\"ما\" مزيدةٌ إبهاميّة، أي: لأمرٍ عظيمٍ يُسَوَّدُ مَن يَسود.\n\nقولُه: (وكفى دليلًا من اعترف)، \"من اعترف\" مفعولُ \"كفى\"، والفاعلُ ما دلَّ عليه لاحتوائها على صفات الله، والضمير في \"بفضلها\" للسورة، و\"صَدَّقَ\" عطفٌ على \"اعترف\"، و\"بقولِ رسولِ الله صلى الله عليه وسلم متعلقٌ بـ\"صَدَّقَ\". وقولُه: أن \"علم التوحيد\" متعلّقٌ بـ\"دليلًا\" وهو تمييز، أي\" كفى ذلك مَن اعترفَ بفضلِ السورة، وصَدَّقَ بقولِ الرسولِ، دليلًا على أن علم التوحيد من الله بمكان.\n\nوالمرادُ بقولِ النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم ما رواه في خاتمة السورة: \"أُسِّستْ السماواتُ السبعُ\" إلى آخره؛ ولم أجدِ الحديثَ في الأصولِ المعتبرة.\n\nوقد وردَ عن الترمذيِّ وأبي داودَ وابن ماجه، عن بريدة، أن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم سمع رجلًا يقول: «اللهم إني أسألك بأني أشهدُ أنك أنت الله، لا إله إلا أنت، الأحدُ الصمد، الذي لم يلدْ ولم يولدْ، ولم يكن له كفوًا أحد. فقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم\" والذي نفسي بيده، لقد سألَ اللهَ باسمِه الأعظم، الذي إذا دُعي به أجاب، وإذا سُئِلَ به أعطى».\n\nقولُه: (فقال: وَجَبَتْ) الحديثُ أخرجه مالكٌ وأحمدُ والترمذيُّ والنسائي عن أبي هريرة.\n\nخاتِمة من كلامِ الشيخ فصيح الدين رحمه الله:\n\nلم يُعطفْ ﴿اللَّهُ الصَّمَدُ﴾ على الجملةِ المتقدمة؛ لأنها محقِّقة لمضمونها ومبينةٌ لها، وكذا ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ﴾؛ لأنها محقِّقةٌ لمضمونِ ﴿اللَّهُ الصَّمَدُ﴾؛ لأن الغني المطلقَ الذي يفتقرُ إليه كل شئٍ لا ينبغي أن يكون والدًا ولا مولودًا؛ لأن ذلك يستلزم الافتقار بالضرورة. وعُطفَ ﴿لَمْ يُولَدْ﴾ على ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ﴾ لأن ﴿لَمْ يُولَدْ﴾ لم يُنبئ عن معنى ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ﴾، فلم يكن محققًا لمعناه، بل الجملتانِ محققتانِ لمضمونِ الجملة السابقة.\n\nوعَطفُ ﴿لَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌ﴾، أن مضمونَها لم يكن محققًا لمضمون السابقتين؛ لأنها تُنبئ عن أنه لا يمكنُ أن يكون له مماثلٌ في شئ مِمّا ذُكِرَ في الذاتِ والصفات، فهو واحدٌ لا شريك له تعالى وتقدّسَ وتَعظّم.\n\nوعُرفَ الخبرُ في ﴿اللَّهُ الصَّمَدُ﴾، نفيًا لنفيِ مَن زعم وسمّى غيرَه صمدًا، ونُكِّرَ في ﴿اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ﴾، لأنهم لم يُسمّوا أشياءًا \"أحدًا\" بهذا المعنى.\n\nتَمّتِ السُّورَة","layers":[{"id":"K112.07","heading":"[K112.07 | T XVI 640–642; K IV 819]","text":"If you ask, “Why is this sūrah the equivalent of the whole Qurʾān, despite its shortness and the closeness of its two ends?”, I answer:\n“It is for some great cause that whoever becomes a master becomes one.”\nThat is solely because it encompasses God's attributes, His justice and His oneness. For anyone who acknowledges its excellence and believes the Messenger of God's ﷺ statement about it, this is sufficient proof that the science of divine oneness (ʿilm (علم) al-tawḥīd) has an exalted standing with God. How could it be otherwise? Knowledge follows its object: it is ennobled by the nobility of what is known, and lowered by its lowliness. The object of this science is God Most High and His attributes, and what is and is not admissible concerning Him. What, then, do you think of the nobility of its rank, the grandeur of its position, its eminence over every other science, and its winning the prize ahead of them? Whoever disparages it does so because of the weakness of his knowledge of its object, the smallness of his reverence for Him, his lack of fear of Him, and his remoteness from considering his own ultimate fate.\nO God, gather us among those who know You, act for You, affirm Your justice and Your oneness, and fear Your threat.\nIt is called “The Sūrah of the Foundation,” because it encompasses the foundations of religion. Ubayy and Anas transmitted from the Prophet ﷺ: “The seven heavens and the seven earths were founded upon ‘Say: He is God, One.’” That is: they were created solely to serve as signs of God's oneness and of the knowledge of His attributes expressed by this sūrah.\nIt is related from the Messenger of God ﷺ that he heard a man reciting “Say: He is God, One,” and said, “It has become assured.” He was asked, “Messenger of God, what has become assured?” He answered, “Paradise has become assured for him.”","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":112,"ar":"فإن قلت: لم كانت هذه السورة عدل القرآن كله على قصر منها وتقارب طرفيها؟ قلت:\n\nلأمر ما يسود من يسود\n\nوما ذاك إلا لاحتوائها على صفات الله تعالى وعدله وتوحيده، وكفى دليلًا من اعترف بفضلها وصدق بقول رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فيها: إنّ علم التوحيد من الله تعالى بمكان، وكيف لا يكون كذلك والعلم تابع للمعلوم: يشرف بشرفه، ويتضع بضعته؛ ومعلوم هذا العلم هو الله تعالى وصفاته، وما يجوز عليه وما لا يجوز، فما ظنك بشرف منزلته وجلالة محله،\n\nوإنافته على كل علم، واستيلائه على قصب السبق دونه؛ ومن ازدراه فلضعف علمه بمعلومة، وقلة تعظيمه له، وخلوه من خشيته، وبعده من النظر لعاقبته. اللهم احشرنا في زمرة العالمين بك العاملين لك، القائلين بعدلك وتوحيدك، الخائفين من وعيدك.\n\nوتسمى \"سورة الأساس\" لاشتمالها على أصول الدين، وروى أبىّ وأنس عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «أسست السماوات السبع والأرضون السبع على قل هو الله أحد»، يعنى ما خلقت إلا لتكون دلائل على توحيد الله ومعرفة صفاته التي نطقت بها هذه السورة.\n\nعن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه سمع رجلا يقرأ: (قل هو الله أحد)، فقال: «وجبت». قيل: يا رسول الله وما \"وجبت\"؟ قال: «وجبت له الجنة».","html":"<p>If you ask, “Why is this sūrah the equivalent of the whole Qurʾān, despite its shortness and the closeness of its two ends?”, I answer:\n“It is for some great cause that whoever becomes a master becomes one.”\nThat is solely because it encompasses God&#39;s attributes, His justice and His oneness. For anyone who acknowledges its excellence and believes the Messenger of God&#39;s ﷺ statement about it, this is sufficient proof that the science of divine oneness (ʿilm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">علم</bdi>) al-tawḥīd) has an exalted standing with God. How could it be otherwise? Knowledge follows its object: it is ennobled by the nobility of what is known, and lowered by its lowliness. The object of this science is God Most High and His attributes, and what is and is not admissible concerning Him. What, then, do you think of the nobility of its rank, the grandeur of its position, its eminence over every other science, and its winning the prize ahead of them? Whoever disparages it does so because of the weakness of his knowledge of its object, the smallness of his reverence for Him, his lack of fear of Him, and his remoteness from considering his own ultimate fate.\nO God, gather us among those who know You, act for You, affirm Your justice and Your oneness, and fear Your threat.\nIt is called “The Sūrah of the Foundation,” because it encompasses the foundations of religion. Ubayy and Anas transmitted from the Prophet ﷺ: “The seven heavens and the seven earths were founded upon ‘Say: He is God, One.’” That is: they were created solely to serve as signs of God&#39;s oneness and of the knowledge of His attributes expressed by this sūrah.\nIt is related from the Messenger of God ﷺ that he heard a man reciting “Say: He is God, One,” and said, “It has become assured.” He was asked, “Messenger of God, what has become assured?” He answered, “Paradise has become assured for him.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.11","heading":"[T112.11 | XVI 640 | on K112.07]","text":"His words, “The equivalent of the whole Qurʾān.” It is transmitted with fatḥa (فَتْحة) or kasra (كَسْرة) on the ʿayn (ع). Al-Akhfash says: “ʿIdl, with kasra (كَسْرة), means an equivalent. ʿAdl, with fatḥa (فَتْحة), is originally the verbal noun in ‘I made this nicely equal [to that]’; you turn it into a noun for an equivalent to distinguish it from a ʿidl (عِدْل), a load of goods.” Al-Farrāʾ says: “ʿAdl, with fatḥa (فَتْحة), is what equals something while belonging to a different kind. ʿIdl, with kasra (كَسْرة), is its like. You say, ‘I have the ʿidl (عِدْل) of your servant-boy, or of your sheep,’ when it is a servant-boy equal to a servant-boy or a sheep equal to a sheep. If you mean its value in something of another kind, you pronounce the ʿayn (ع) with fatḥa (فَتْحة). Some Arabs sometimes pronounce it with kasra (كَسْرة); that was an error on their part.”","title":"“The equivalent of the whole Qurʾān”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.07"],"anchor":"K112.07","surah":112,"ar":"قولُه: (عَدْلَ القرآنِ كلِّه)، يُروى بفتح العين وكسرها، قال الأخفش: العِدْلُ بالكسر: المِثْل، والعَدْلُ بالفتح: أصلُه مصدر قولك: عَدَلْتَ بهذا عَدْلًا حسنًا، تجعله اسمًا للمِثْلِ، لِتُفْرُق بينه وبين عِدْل المتاع. وقال الفرّاء: العَدْلُ بالفتح: ما عادلَ الشّئ من غير جنسه، والعِدْلُ بالكسر: المِثْل. وتقول: عندي عِدْلُ غلامك، وعِدْلُ شاتك، إذا كان غلامًا يعدلُ غلامًا، أو شاةً تعدلُ شاة، فإذا أردت قيمته من غير جنسه، نَصبتَ العين، وربّما كَسَرها بعضُ العرب، وكان منهم غلط.","html":"<p>His words, “The equivalent of the whole Qurʾān.” It is transmitted with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>) or kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) on the ʿayn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ع</bdi>). Al-Akhfash says: “ʿIdl, with kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>), means an equivalent. ʿAdl, with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>), is originally the verbal noun in ‘I made this nicely equal [to that]’; you turn it into a noun for an equivalent to distinguish it from a ʿidl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عِدْل</bdi>), a load of goods.” Al-Farrāʾ says: “ʿAdl, with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>), is what equals something while belonging to a different kind. ʿIdl, with kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>), is its like. You say, ‘I have the ʿidl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عِدْل</bdi>) of your servant-boy, or of your sheep,’ when it is a servant-boy equal to a servant-boy or a sheep equal to a sheep. If you mean its value in something of another kind, you pronounce the ʿayn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ع</bdi>) with fatḥa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَتْحة</bdi>). Some Arabs sometimes pronounce it with kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>); that was an error on their part.”</p>","lemma":"عدل القرآن كله","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.12","heading":"[T112.12 | XVI 640–641 | on K112.07]","text":"The correct version is “one third of the Qurʾān.” We have transmitted from al-Bukhārī, Muslim, Mālik, Abū Dāwūd and al-Nasāʾī, from Abū Saʿīd, that a man heard another reciting “Say: He is God, One” repeatedly. In the morning he came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned it, apparently regarding it as little. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, it is indeed equivalent to one third of the Qurʾān.”\nAl-Qāḍī says: “Because this sūrah, short as it is, encompasses all knowledge of divinity and refutes those who deviate concerning it, the report says that it equals one third of the Qurʾān. The Qurʾān's aims are confined to explaining beliefs, rulings and narratives. Whoever equates it with the whole takes into account what is intended for its own sake among these.”\nHis words, “It is for some cause that whoever becomes a master becomes one”: the opening is:\n“I resolved to stay until morning.”\nMā is an additional particle giving indefiniteness and magnification: that is, it is for a great cause that whoever becomes a master becomes one.\n[SOURCE CHECK T112-U3: Al-Baghdādī, Khizānat al-adab III:86–88, records the full line and explains إقامة ذي صباح as remaining until morning. The rendering follows that explanation; it does not establish every detail of the poem's attribution or context.]","title":"“It is for some cause that whoever becomes a master becomes one”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.07"],"anchor":"K112.07","surah":112,"ar":"والصحيحُ: ثلثُ القرآن؛ رَوينا عن البخاريّ ومسلم ومالك وأبي داودَ والنسائي، عن أبي سعيد، أن رجلًا سمعَ رجلًا يقرأ: ﴿قُلْ هُوَ اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ﴾ يردّدها، فلما أصبحَ جاءَ إلى النبيّ صلى الله عليه وسلم، فذكرَ ذلك له، وكأنّ الرجلَ يتقالُّها، فقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «والذي نفسي بيده، إنها لتعدلُ ثُلُثَ القرآن».\n\nقال القاضي: «ولاشتمال هذه السورة مع قِصرِها على جميع المعارفِ الإلهية، والردِّ على مَن ألحدَ فيها، جاء في الحديثِ أنها تعدلُ ثلثَ القرآن، لأن مقاصدِ القرآنِ محصورةٌ في بيانِ العقائد، والأحكام، والقصص، ومَن عَدَلَها بكلِّه اعتبرَ المقصودِ بالذاتِ من ذلك».\n\nقولُه: (لأمرٍ ما يُسَوَّدُ مَن يَسودُ)، أوله:\n\nعَزَمتُ على إقامةِ ذي صباحِ\n\nو\"ما\" مزيدةٌ إبهاميّة، أي: لأمرٍ عظيمٍ يُسَوَّدُ مَن يَسود.","html":"<p>The correct version is “one third of the Qurʾān.” We have transmitted from al-Bukhārī, Muslim, Mālik, Abū Dāwūd and al-Nasāʾī, from Abū Saʿīd, that a man heard another reciting “Say: He is God, One” repeatedly. In the morning he came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned it, apparently regarding it as little. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, it is indeed equivalent to one third of the Qurʾān.”\nAl-Qāḍī says: “Because this sūrah, short as it is, encompasses all knowledge of divinity and refutes those who deviate concerning it, the report says that it equals one third of the Qurʾān. The Qurʾān&#39;s aims are confined to explaining beliefs, rulings and narratives. Whoever equates it with the whole takes into account what is intended for its own sake among these.”\nHis words, “It is for some cause that whoever becomes a master becomes one”: the opening is:\n“I resolved to stay until morning.”\nMā is an additional particle giving indefiniteness and magnification: that is, it is for a great cause that whoever becomes a master becomes one.\n[SOURCE CHECK T112-U3: Al-Baghdādī, Khizānat al-adab III:86–88, records the full line and explains <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إقامة ذي صباح</bdi> as remaining until morning. The rendering follows that explanation; it does not establish every detail of the poem&#39;s attribution or context.]</p>","lemma":"لأمر ما يسود من يسود","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.13","heading":"[T112.13 | XVI 641 | on K112.07]","text":"His words, “It suffices as proof for whoever acknowledges.” Man iʿtarafa [whoever acknowledges] is the object of kafā (كفى) [suffices]. Its subject is what is indicated by “because it encompasses God's attributes.” The pronoun in “its excellence” refers to the sūrah. “Believes” is coordinated with “acknowledges”; “the Messenger of God's ﷺ statement” attaches to “believes.” “That the science of divine oneness” attaches to “as proof,” which is a specification (tamyīz (تمييز)). That is: for whoever acknowledges the sūrah's excellence and believes the Messenger's statement, this suffices as proof that the science of divine oneness has an exalted standing with God.\nThe Prophet's ﷺ statement intended here is the one he transmits at the end of the sūrah: “The seven heavens were founded …,” to the end. I have not found this report in the recognized source collections.\nAl-Tirmidhī, Abū Dāwūd and Ibn Māja transmit from Burayda that the Messenger of God ﷺ heard a man saying: “O God, I ask You by my testimony that You are God—there is no god but You—the One, al-Ṣamad, who neither begot nor was begotten, and to whom no one is equal.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, he has asked God by His greatest name: when invoked by it, He answers; when asked by it, He gives.”","title":"“It suffices as proof for whoever acknowledges”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.07"],"anchor":"K112.07","surah":112,"ar":"قولُه: (وكفى دليلًا من اعترف)، \"من اعترف\" مفعولُ \"كفى\"، والفاعلُ ما دلَّ عليه لاحتوائها على صفات الله، والضمير في \"بفضلها\" للسورة، و\"صَدَّقَ\" عطفٌ على \"اعترف\"، و\"بقولِ رسولِ الله صلى الله عليه وسلم متعلقٌ بـ\"صَدَّقَ\". وقولُه: أن \"علم التوحيد\" متعلّقٌ بـ\"دليلًا\" وهو تمييز، أي\" كفى ذلك مَن اعترفَ بفضلِ السورة، وصَدَّقَ بقولِ الرسولِ، دليلًا على أن علم التوحيد من الله بمكان.\n\nوالمرادُ بقولِ النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم ما رواه في خاتمة السورة: \"أُسِّستْ السماواتُ السبعُ\" إلى آخره؛ ولم أجدِ الحديثَ في الأصولِ المعتبرة.\n\nوقد وردَ عن الترمذيِّ وأبي داودَ وابن ماجه، عن بريدة، أن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم سمع رجلًا يقول: «اللهم إني أسألك بأني أشهدُ أنك أنت الله، لا إله إلا أنت، الأحدُ الصمد، الذي لم يلدْ ولم يولدْ، ولم يكن له كفوًا أحد. فقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم\" والذي نفسي بيده، لقد سألَ اللهَ باسمِه الأعظم، الذي إذا دُعي به أجاب، وإذا سُئِلَ به أعطى».","html":"<p>His words, “It suffices as proof for whoever acknowledges.” Man iʿtarafa [whoever acknowledges] is the object of kafā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كفى</bdi>) [suffices]. Its subject is what is indicated by “because it encompasses God&#39;s attributes.” The pronoun in “its excellence” refers to the sūrah. “Believes” is coordinated with “acknowledges”; “the Messenger of God&#39;s ﷺ statement” attaches to “believes.” “That the science of divine oneness” attaches to “as proof,” which is a specification (tamyīz (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تمييز</bdi>)). That is: for whoever acknowledges the sūrah&#39;s excellence and believes the Messenger&#39;s statement, this suffices as proof that the science of divine oneness has an exalted standing with God.\nThe Prophet&#39;s ﷺ statement intended here is the one he transmits at the end of the sūrah: “The seven heavens were founded …,” to the end. I have not found this report in the recognized source collections.\nAl-Tirmidhī, Abū Dāwūd and Ibn Māja transmit from Burayda that the Messenger of God ﷺ heard a man saying: “O God, I ask You by my testimony that You are God—there is no god but You—the One, al-Ṣamad, who neither begot nor was begotten, and to whom no one is equal.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, he has asked God by His greatest name: when invoked by it, He answers; when asked by it, He gives.”</p>","lemma":"وكفى دليلا من اعترف","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.14","heading":"[T112.14 | XVI 642 | on K112.07]","text":"His words, “He said: It has become assured.” Mālik, Aḥmad, al-Tirmidhī and al-Nasāʾī transmitted the report from Abū Hurayra.","title":"“He said: It has become assured”","author":"tibi","on":["K112.07"],"anchor":"K112.07","surah":112,"ar":"قولُه: (فقال: وَجَبَتْ) الحديثُ أخرجه مالكٌ وأحمدُ والترمذيُّ والنسائي عن أبي هريرة.","html":"<p>His words, “He said: It has become assured.” Mālik, Aḥmad, al-Tirmidhī and al-Nasāʾī transmitted the report from Abū Hurayra.</p>","lemma":"فقال: وجبت","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T112.15","heading":"[T112.15 | XVI 642–643 | concluding addition]","text":"A conclusion from the words of Shaykh Faṣīḥ al-Dīn—may God have mercy on him:\n“‘God, al-Ṣamad’ is not joined by a conjunction to the preceding sentence, because it confirms and explains its content. The same applies to ‘He did not beget,’ because it confirms the content of ‘God, al-Ṣamad’: the absolutely self-sufficient one, whom everything needs, cannot be a begetter or begotten, since that necessarily entails need.\n“‘Nor was He begotten’ is joined to ‘He did not beget,’ because ‘was not begotten’ does not express the meaning of ‘did not beget,’ and thus does not confirm its meaning; rather, the two sentences confirm the content of the preceding sentence. ‘And no one is equal to Him’ is joined [by a conjunction] because its content does not confirm the content of the two preceding sentences: it expresses that He cannot have any equal in anything mentioned concerning essence and attributes. He is therefore one, with no partner—exalted, holy and supremely great.\n“The predicate is definite in ‘God, al-Ṣamad’ to negate [the claim of] anyone who asserts and names another as ṣamad (صمد). It is indefinite in ‘God, One,’ because they did not call things aḥad (أحد) in this sense.”\n[REVIEW T112-U4: The scan reads نفيا لنفي من زعم وسمى غيره صمدا, literally “to negate the negation of whoever asserted and named another ṣamad (صمد).” This double negation is problematic. The bracketed rendering gives the apparent intended argument, not a verified emendation. Retain the flag pending another witness or the quoted source.]\nThe sūrah is complete.","title":"Commentary","author":"tibi","on":["K112.07"],"anchor":"K112.07","surah":112,"ar":"خاتِمة من كلامِ الشيخ فصيح الدين رحمه الله:\n\nلم يُعطفْ ﴿اللَّهُ الصَّمَدُ﴾ على الجملةِ المتقدمة؛ لأنها محقِّقة لمضمونها ومبينةٌ لها، وكذا ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ﴾؛ لأنها محقِّقةٌ لمضمونِ ﴿اللَّهُ الصَّمَدُ﴾؛ لأن الغني المطلقَ الذي يفتقرُ إليه كل شئٍ لا ينبغي أن يكون والدًا ولا مولودًا؛ لأن ذلك يستلزم الافتقار بالضرورة. وعُطفَ ﴿لَمْ يُولَدْ﴾ على ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ﴾ لأن ﴿لَمْ يُولَدْ﴾ لم يُنبئ عن معنى ﴿لَمْ يَلِدْ﴾، فلم يكن محققًا لمعناه، بل الجملتانِ محققتانِ لمضمونِ الجملة السابقة.\n\nوعَطفُ ﴿لَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌ﴾، أن مضمونَها لم يكن محققًا لمضمون السابقتين؛ لأنها تُنبئ عن أنه لا يمكنُ أن يكون له مماثلٌ في شئ مِمّا ذُكِرَ في الذاتِ والصفات، فهو واحدٌ لا شريك له تعالى وتقدّسَ وتَعظّم.\n\nوعُرفَ الخبرُ في ﴿اللَّهُ الصَّمَدُ﴾، نفيًا لنفيِ مَن زعم وسمّى غيرَه صمدًا، ونُكِّرَ في ﴿اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ﴾، لأنهم لم يُسمّوا أشياءًا \"أحدًا\" بهذا المعنى.\n\nتَمّتِ السُّورَة","html":"<p>A conclusion from the words of Shaykh Faṣīḥ al-Dīn—may God have mercy on him:\n“‘God, al-Ṣamad’ is not joined by a conjunction to the preceding sentence, because it confirms and explains its content. The same applies to ‘He did not beget,’ because it confirms the content of ‘God, al-Ṣamad’: the absolutely self-sufficient one, whom everything needs, cannot be a begetter or begotten, since that necessarily entails need.\n“‘Nor was He begotten’ is joined to ‘He did not beget,’ because ‘was not begotten’ does not express the meaning of ‘did not beget,’ and thus does not confirm its meaning; rather, the two sentences confirm the content of the preceding sentence. ‘And no one is equal to Him’ is joined [by a conjunction] because its content does not confirm the content of the two preceding sentences: it expresses that He cannot have any equal in anything mentioned concerning essence and attributes. He is therefore one, with no partner—exalted, holy and supremely great.\n“The predicate is definite in ‘God, al-Ṣamad’ to negate [the claim of] anyone who asserts and names another as ṣamad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صمد</bdi>). It is indefinite in ‘God, One,’ because they did not call things aḥad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحد</bdi>) in this sense.”\n[REVIEW T112-U4: The scan reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">نفيا لنفي من زعم وسمى غيره صمدا</bdi>, literally “to negate the negation of whoever asserted and named another ṣamad (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صمد</bdi>).” This double negation is problematic. The bracketed rendering gives the apparent intended argument, not a verified emendation. Retain the flag pending another witness or the quoted source.]\nThe sūrah is complete.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"112:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ","en":"Say: He is Allah, the One"},{"key":"112:2","number":2,"ar":"ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ","en":"Allah, the eternally Besought of all"},{"key":"112:3","number":3,"ar":"لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ","en":"He begetteth not nor was begotten"},{"key":"112:4","number":4,"ar":"وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ","en":"And there is none comparable unto Him"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ ﴿1﴾ ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ ﴿2﴾ لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ ﴿3﴾ وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: He is Allah, the One 2. Allah, the eternally Besought of all 3. He begetteth not nor was begotten 4. And there is none comparable unto Him"}];
